e oil, so I say many authorities, taken in qnantities, secures both health and beauty, hut it is possible that many of us , would consider the result to be dear at the pri4. Then , there are disciples of the orange and lemon treatment, the former being applied internally, and the latter externally. The majority however, forbid alcohol in any shape or. form, and agree that barley water is superior to lemonade as a beauty drink. The Gospel of Open-air Life.—Advocates of the open air life tell us that we do not know " the wild, joy of -living "until we adopt the practice of sleeping out of doors. That may be so, hut unfortunately our houses are not as a rule built upon the bungalow system, and unless we resort to the fields or hedgerows, or the back garden, few of us have facilities for sleeping out of . • , doors even if we wished to do so. In America we read of upstairs balconies being turned into " sleeping porches," and of mothers putting their children out to sleep in suspended cradles fromfifteen er twenty storied ; "sky scrapers " and of others who sleep with their beads out of window with the rest of their body inside. It is said that people who adopt such a life for no other ' reeson than that of general health, never have a cold or ailment, but possibly it is only the very strong who survive, and it is difficult to avoid the impression that these faddists run great risks and put up with much discomfort, without obtaining any benefits beyond that afforded by open windows, and a well ventilatad bed- room. A Gipsy Invasion.—The eipsy, however picturesque is not a very desirable product of open-air life, and he promises to become a great nuisance in the country this summer. Germany is abolishing the gipsy, and by a new law he has either to give up his vagabond life, and take up a settled residence, or be expelled the country. That is why so many are flocking over, nnd it is said that unless a check, is put upon them, there will soon 'be many hundreds, if not thousands of these foreign</p>
