      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-05_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>WARRINGTON DOCK.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON DOCK.</p>
        <p>ALDERMAN BENNETT AND CORPORATION APATHY. Ship Canal Company's Offer.</p>
        <p>The question of the full utilization of the facilities for water carriage offered by the Manchester Ship Canal was raised at the monthly meeting of the War- rington Town Council on Tuesday when a lengthy report was presented by Alderman Bennett and Coun- cillor Fulton Smith upon an interview with the Manchester Ship Canal Comptny with a view to induc- ing the company to agree to an extension of time for , the construction of a dock at Warrington. Commenting upon the report Alderman BENNETT said the Rivers Committee had visited Walton lay-by but he was sorry the attendance of the committee when they visited the lay-by was mat by any means as satis- factory as he would have liked, and it was typical of the ektreme apathy with which he was sorry to say the Corporation had viewed the question from the outset. On many occasions he had been impressed with the short- sightedness of the Corporation but of all thiega which stood to their discredit, from want of appreciation of its importance the question of the Warrington Dock WWI perhaps the most appalling. Ever since he entered the Council he had been Asking them to appreciate its importance and at last he had succeeded in getting the committee to visit the site involved. Some members of the committee went and some did not and those who did not he thought had failed in their duty. Those who did go w:.re favourably impressed with what they saw and realized that there was a good deal anore in the ques- tion than sortie members of the Council were apt to think. He and .Counciller Fulton Smith had also bad an interview with the Ship Canal Company and had been received in a courteous, fair, and reasonable spirit. The position as •ai the future rested with the Corporation and if they continued to go on in that apathetic, indifferent, sleepy way and continued to show an incapacity to look beyond their own noses they would he tcablarne, as they were allowed to see quite clearly that if Warrington were alive to its opportunities they would not meet with any serieus opposition from the Ship Canal Campany. More than that the Company promised to give them help. All that they required to do NiTS to show the Company that there was traffic that required accommodation, and they would assist in giving the town facilities. The alarm- ing part of the whole position eas that, owing to the way in which years slipped by without anything being done, the opportunities afforded them under the original proposals were slipping away, ana the deputation were made painfully aware that.the whole of the site for the dock was rf.pidly being absorbed by the mud dredged from, the river. He was not going to say enything about dredging as it was still in a sense sub-judice, but what- ever advantage dredging might be, it was eet,remendous disadvantage as the mud taken from the river covered up the site available for a dock. He suggested to the Company the desirability of the site toing allowed 16 remain open and asked whether there was any way of obtaining an alternative site, but the Company's repre- sentatives said there was nothing for it but to put the mud on the meadows. Mr. Hunter told them that in four or five years the whole of the meadows would be covered by trfmendous mountains of mud. Whether Warrington had sold its birthright for a mess of pottage he could not say but from the point of view of the interests of the whole town,in the interest of a certain section of the town and of a few traders who were foAnnate Enough to be situated by the river it was quite possible that tie dock originally contemplated would have been of more substantial service to the town than what had been obtained under another head. At present it was out of the question to construct a doca. He woeld not say that was due to the Corporation's wave of economy as even some of the econondits weie in ravel. r of a dock. but it would be hopeless at present to ask the people to embark on a great enterprise of that character. The thin end of the wedge had now been introduced. A shed had been built by the lay-by and near the trans- porter which was built at their own request. The shed was sufficient as an experiment, mid if in future they did not use the lay-by and take advantage of the prox- imity of the canal it would not be the fault of • the Canal Company. The Chamber of Commerce was seek- ing to bring about the utilization of the lay-by and enquiries which had been made were highly satisfactory as a large number of prominent local firms had agreed to support the scheme. It was extremely probable that three a- lines of steamers—from Belfast,' London and Glasgow—would shortly be stopping at the lay-by for goods. There was no doubt that if they could get the nucleus of sufficient traffic in that shed that, by and by, other steamers would call also. If the do ck was not immediately practicable, if they bad not the energy, foresight, wisdom, economy or no ' economy, to provide those facilities, they wanted I , the opportunity to provide them later on if the traffic developed. He urged every gentleman in the Corpora- tion and every manufacturer to put his back into the work and give every possible support to the promoters of the scheme,and to gradually assist in the creaticn of I facilities forincreased traffic which could notlitil to be } of advantage to the town, to the Ship Canal Company, I and to everybody concerned. If they backed this small I initial traffic up and found they were able to fill the, shed, the Ship Canal Company would build a larger , shed, and the Company had given them in a frank nod straightforward manner the assurances that if the ; Corporation approached them in time with a practice suggestipn for the creation of greater facilities, they would meet the Corporation in a friendly spirit and do their best to help them. (Hear, hear.) Councillor BURTON did not think the people of War- rington should be called upon te find the money. No i doubt the dock would he a great benefit, but if the rates went up in proportion they would he better with- out it. If they had the rates too high they kept manu- facturers away, and drove away those who were in business here. There was only one way in which- their I prosperity could he kept up and that was by keeping , the rates down. (Hear, hear.) ICouncillor PEMBERTON said he was glad to be able to support Alderman Bennett, but he would apply the principles of economy to the dock. His idea of economy was a plain, strong and useful dock and if Alderman Bennett wanted to build a dock with beautiful orna- mented iron columns with a garden at the top he would fight him tooth and Nil. (Laughter.) f Alderman BENNETT My present idea is to use 1 simply piles. (LenehterO Continuing Col/miller PEMBERTON said his economy I was is business economy—that they should not spend 30s. where £1 would do. Ile did not think the inhabit- ants of the town or the members of the Council realized the benefit which water carriage would be to Warrington, but they must have the dock before the traffic would come. We made almost everything in Warrington, and if we had a dock we should have more shipping tradeoban a great many people imagined. They had an old river bed, and they had only to form one side to be going on with. The dock %vas all a question of terms, and if Warrington had to spend the money, it slould have the benefit of it. Certainly the matter was worth their most serious consideration. It was unfortunate they had got across with the Ship Canal Company, but they should not allow that to blind their judgment as a Corporation, and as business men. He supested that a large committee be formed to inquire into the question. Councillor EVANS said the Ship Canal Company were doing a good deal to bring trade to Manchester, hut he had not heard that they had seriously considered War- rington. es a Corporation they ought to have a proper understanding with the company as to the conditions of trade between them. (Hear, hear.) The important qmstion of dock does would have to be considered. Ho knew the company were anxious to get all the trade they could up to the poit of Manchester. The MAYOR said that if the company had built the dock according to the Act of Parliament it would have been a great boon to warrington, but until the dues were arranged he did not think it was worth while -to consider whether a dock should be built or not. The great question was whether they could get the traffic into the dock at such a small charge that traders would Justified in making use of it. The minutes were approved.</p>
