g o poeurt_ 'osition of Alderman BENpaT, seconded by Councillor ' ot deasoN. 1 vision of the roads ; the Electricity and Tram- ways Committee to immediately commence the repairs of the roads of whidh they have had notice from the Borough Surveyor, and in the event of their not complying with such notice the Borough Surveyor to have the power to execute the work at the cost of the Electricity and Tramways Committee; the Borough Surveyor to retain power at any time to carry out any work which in his opinion is dangerous, and to charge the Electricity and Trarnways Committee with the cost of same ; the Electricity and Tramways Com- mittee to accept responsibility for all places where repairs are necessary and for the carrying out of such repairs; when an order has been given by the Electricity and Tramways Committee to the contractor for any repairs to be executed a duplicate of sech order to be sent to the Borough Surveyor." Tke minutes were approved. . The Electricity and Tramways Committee reported the acceptance of the tender of Mr. William Oven for the repair of the tramway tans for six months accord- ing to specification and schedule of prices, hilt ate sub- sequent meeting Mr. Owen declined the contract and it was decided to invite tenders for the work and that Pending the acceptance of a tender the Eugineer be authorized to carry out such repairs as he considers necessary. Councillor WILKINSON (in theaabsence of Alderman Smethurst) moved the cones-elation of the minutes. ; Councillor PEACOCK seconded. Courmillor STOTT moved that the minute with regard to the upkeep of the tramway track be not confirmed. , Ho thought tliat seeing they had such a , splendidly equipped paving depertment and after the ' object lessees they had had with regard to the condition of the tramway% they ought not to take the work out of the hands of the committee and give it to a private contractor. The cost of repaving the tramways had already amounted to nearly El,oeo which he was assured on good authority was twice the amount they ought to have spent. If the Corporation did ' the 'work they could guarantee that it a ould be well done and although the cost might be more at the outset, it would be cheaper in the long run. I Alderman Briaterr seconded.. Alderman TINNION said there had been a great deal of bickering and quarrelling as to the accounte sent in by ' the Paving and Sewerage Committee for work done. and for the sake of peace and quietness he had taken off 5 Per cent. and sometimes 10 per cent for cash. (Laughter.) It had been a burning question whether the Paving and Sewage Committee were doing the work as well and as cheaply nun an outside contractor would do it, but that committee would be obliged to Councillor Stott if he would not press the matter. The work must be done</p>
