      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-05_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, May 5, 1906.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, May 5, 1906.</p>
        <p>to their satisfaction and under their inspection, and it was only a question of getting the friction between the committees settled. They really felt there had been no satisfaction in doing the work, although they submitted that anything they had done would bear investigation. Under the new conditions the committee would not give up their rights. He thltrght Alderman Bennett must be paying off an old score in seconding the amend- ment. (Laughter.) Alderman BENNETT f No. I have done it in further- ance of a principle I have advocated for a long time. Councillor WILKINSON said the Tramways Committee had felt for sometime that they were paying heavily for the work done by the Paving and Sewerage Committee. He (the speaker) would not municipalize all things. He was more in favour of honest competition in any' com- munity. They had felt for sometime that they were paying rather two \much for their repairs, and that repairs were modest times that were not required. Were the committee not adopting the proper course in getting outside help and getting the work done cheaper? They were economists strictly. (Laughter.) They were endeavouring to see that the Tramways Committee was not overcharged for any work that was done. (Hear, hear.) Councillor PEYIREILTON said he would support the minutes. The Tramways Committee had had to pay large sums of money. He could not see what difference the resolution would make from Mr. Stott's point of view. The labour employed would be Warrington labour, and it was not to be let to an outsider. This was only an experiment. Councillor STOTT said that after the explanations which had been given he would withdraw his amend- ment.</p>
        <p>Alderman BENNETT concurred, and the minutes were' passed.</p>
        <p>TRAMWAY MATTERS.</p>
        <p>The Electricit-y-Ucf.T7a-mCv;;; -a-c:UI--Mittee resolved, "That the system of workmen's return tickets in use of the Wilderspool route be extended to the other routes where workmen's cars are run." _ .</p>
        <p>It was reported that the sanction of the Local Government Board to the borrowing of £7,00 for cables and services had been received." A letter from the Warrington Tradesmen's Associa- tion asking the committee ttrNextend the tramways along Sankey-street to Legtbatreet was read. Resolved f` That the association be informed that the Corporation have no powers to make such extension." Councillor WILSON having given notice of his intention to move a resolution at the next meeting of the Council to allow tramway men and policemen when in uniform to travel free on the cars, and a petition from 41 car drivers and conductors asking, the com- mittee to reconsider the question having bean r: after fully considering the matter it was resolved, "that tramway men and policemen when in uniform be allowed to travel bn the cars free of charge." Resolved, "that the Town Clerk apply to the Board of Trade for an order prolonging the time for the completion of the tramways. ' Resolved, that the Tovia Clerk apply to the Board of Trade for on order prolonging the time, for the purchase of lands for, and the construction of Light Railway No. 2." The minutes were approved. EXTENSION OF THE INFIRMARY. Ate meeting of the General Purposes Committee an application from the Governors of the Infirmary for a donation towards the fund being raised for extending the Infirmary was read and deferred. The minutes were agreed to without comment on the motion of the M.tvoil, seconded by the DEPUTY MAYOR.</p>
        <p>TENDERS ACCEPTED. SANITARY WORKS. Messrs. W. and J. ttichardson, for a six months' supply of slack according to sample. The offer of the London Electron Works to purchase tin refuse in the following quantities and prices, !mini- mum consignments of tceo tons, 15s. per ton ; minimum consignments of four tons, 19s. per ton. BOWLS. Messrs. G. and T. Royle, Manchester, of ten sets of bowls at .C 5 us. 3d. GAS COMMITTEE. Messrs. Westwood and Wright, of West Bromwich for the supply of valves, according to schedule of prices. WATER COMMITTEE. The Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Co., Ltd. for the dismantling of the Beam Engine Water Lily, at Winwick Pumping Station according to specification at £7B.</p>
        <p>TRAMWAYS AND ELECTRICITY. Messrs. W. and J. Richardson, for the supply of slack for 12 months.</p>
        <p>LOAN. The Sanitary Works Committee report the receipt of the approval of the Local Government Board to the borrowing of the sum of £1,200 for the construction of additional bacteria bed at Longford Depot. These were all all agreed to. . CONSTIPATION.</p>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>SUNIMARY OF RESULTS. NORTHERN UNION CUP.-FLIAL.</p>
        <p>G. T. P. G. T. P. Bradford NORTHERN RUGBY LEAGUE. Dewsbury Millom 2 2 10 Pontefract -1 5 Warrington 3 3 15 Hunslet l-0 2 Broughton R. ... 5 8 34 Widnes -1 5 Halifax 2 1 7 York -0 2 Barrow 0 0 0 Leigh O-0 0</p>
        <p>• Warrtngton Finish Strongly. The football season concluded at Warrington on Saturday,with the match with Hunslet, and the 4,000 spectators present had the pleasure of seeing the homesters in their best form. It was a very important engagement for the visitors,who, if they had won,would have been League champions and they came with every determination to succeed. Warrington were without Preston, Isherwood and Hockenhall but the reserves could not have been improved upon and Hunslet had to bow the knee to a decidedly better team. Rather stormy weather prevailed, and the ground got very wet as the result of a heavy downpour of sleet in the first half. Mr. Clegg of Manningham was the referee, and the teams were as follow :</p>
        <p>Warrington.—Berry, back; Fish, Taylor, Dickenson, and Jordan, three-quarter backs ; Lees and Brooks, half backs ; Preston, Boardman, Thomas, Shugars, Swift, Hamer, Belton, and Heath, forwards. Hunslet.—Place, back ; Ward,Eagers, W. Goldthorpe, and Jackson, three-quarter backs ; A. Goldthorpe, and Everson, half backs ; Shooter, Brookes, Randall, Dyson, Walsh, Wray, Wilson, and Jukes, forwards. Playing against the breeze Hunslet were early com- pelled to resort to purely defensive tactics, and twice in, quick succession Place was compelled to save. Strong forward work by Shooter and Wray, seconded by clever kicking by the brothers Goldthorpe, gave the visitors the advantage, but Berry with a huge punt placed Warrington on the attack, and a minute later the home custodian missed placing a goal by inches. Hunslet again had the better of the game at close quarters, and several sharp rushes caused Berry trouble. Eagers and Uohnson in turn tried hard to force their way over, and eventually Lees got well away, and Taylor sending °n to Fish, that player raced speedily along touch, and being challenged by Ward punted in the mouth of goal. Place saved, but from the pack which followed Lees dropped- a smart goal. Hunslet got dangerously near on resuming, but there was little sting in their attack, and loose passing placed Fish in possession, and punting over Place's head he left all behind in a long race to the line and scored at the corner, Hunslet failed to make headway, and to add to their discomfiture, a blinding hailstorm swept over the ground. From some smart play by Lees, Taylor got away, and kicking to the open Heath scored at his leisure behind the posts, this point being augmented by Fish. Half-time—Warrington 2 goals and 2 tries (10 points), Hunslet nil.</p>
        <p>Witli. the elements in their favour Hunslet opened the second half full of promise, and in quick succession Jackson and Eagers were held up near the line. A. Goldthorpe failed at a penalty, and Warrington immedi- ately rushed to the centre of the field, where Fish was held up at the .q)iarter flag after he had practically ' cleared al! opposition. The home men forced the pace, and after Fish and Jordan had been checked near the corner flags, a hastly visit to the other end witnessed W. Goldthorpe experience hard luck after a really brilliant dribble. Hunslet fell away after this, and the the leaders showing brilliant form Lees put on the finishing touch to a smart exhibition of passing by the forwards. Fish added a goal. Albert Goldthorpe placed a penalty goal, and from this point even play was the order to the end. Result :—Warrington, 3 goals and 3 tries (15 points), Hunslet, 1 goal (2 points).</p>
        <p>-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-05_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>MOST SUCCESSFUL REMEDY IN OBSTINATE AND HABITUAL CASES IS VENDS SEAWEED TONIC. IT IS VASTLY SUPERIOR TO PILLS OF ANY KIND.</head>
        <p>THE MOST SUCCESSFUL REMEDY IN OBSTINATE AND HABITUAL CASES IS VENDS SEAWEED TONIC. IT IS VASTLY SUPERIOR TO PILLS OF ANY KIND.</p>
