      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-05_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>BOWLING.</head>
        <p>BOWLING.</p>
        <p>COUNTY CHAMPIONSHIP CO ?,IPE rmoN.</p>
        <p>The representative council of the Lancashire and Cheshire Association met in Manchester on Tuesday evening, to make the draw for the eighteenth annual contest for the team championship of the two counties. All the matches to be played prior to the 15th of May. The following is the draw : Northenden v. Warrington (Cemetery), at Nelson, Urmston. Victoria, Urmston, v. Broughton, at Northenden. Knutsford v. Reddish, at Chorlton. Pendleton v. Bowden, at Northumberland Arms. Chorlton v. Belmont, at Withington. Urmston (Lord Nelson) v. Lymm, at Warrington.</p>
        <p>Didshury v. Justice Bir,ch, Gorton, at Prestwich. Northumberland v. Trafford (Friday), at Middleton Junction. , Oughtrington Park v. Flixton Roebuck, at Justice ; Birch, Gorton. Kersal Moor v. Newton, at Victoria, Urmston. Kersal George v. Winnington Park, at Griffin,Bowdon. Heaton iMersey v. Leigh Railway, at Heatley and Warburton. Middleton Junction v. Cheatham Temple, at Kenai Moor. Mani v. Leigh Limited, at Temple.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-05_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>INDIGESTION GORED.</head>
        <p>INDIGESTION GORED.</p>
        <p>A Waithamstow Woman's Recognition of Benefit Derived from Mother Seigel's Syrup.</p>
        <p>• ; Mrs. Greenwood, of 1.61, ItingwOod •Road, Waltham- stow,relates an experienco of suaering which is all too common, and would be almost universal but for the good work done by Mother Seigel's Syrup. Since 1368 (thirty-seven years ago !) this world-renowned remedy has been known as the greatest cure for indigestion and biliousness; and for the numerous complaints which ; srise from those two ailments when neglected. "I cannot refrain from writing to tell you of the benefit I have , derived from taking Mother Seigel's Syrup," says Mrs. i Greenwood in ,a letter dated April 12th, 1905. " For quite a long time I suffered from indigestion. ;My appetite was extremely poor; and though ! sometimes I felt as if I could eat a little, no sooner ! was food placed before me than the desire passed, and I would turn away; leaving it untouched. When indeed I did eat anything, I was alwtys punished by great pain in the stomach and a sensation of pressure at the chest immediately afterwards. Sometimes there was also pain at the back between the shoulders." Those are the ordinary symptoms of indigestion, and they constitute an illness in themselves. But, unfor- tunately,there are others. Mrs. Greenwood continues : "The headaches I endured were agonising. They were not like ordinary headaches, but infinitely more severe, I and I was hardly over free from them. Another of my troubles was flatulence. . .</p>
        <p>„.,._ "I tried a great number of proprietary medicines and when all these failed, placed myself under the care of a doctor. But I derived no benefit whatever from either one or the other. On the contrary, my condition steadily became worse until the day I thought of taking Mother Seigel 's Curative Syrup. Then,for the first time.l began to mend. The headaches became less frequent and violent, and soon after ceased altogether; my appeal° and strength returned, and ilia short time I was my old self again, fully restored to health.”</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-05_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>WOMAN'S REALM.</head>
        <p>WOMAN'S REALM.</p>
        <p>DRESS-FASI. lON-1-10111E.</p>
        <p>HATS IN PARIS.</p>
        <p>The milliners of Paris have with one accord started a season of feathers of every imaginable shade, shape, and form. These vary from plumes to peacocks' feathers, aigrettes, or orna- ments composed of many feathers which have no specific name. All the spring hats will be gay with flowers, the newest of which promise to be of unnatural form and colouring. But for the moment feathers reign supreme, end it is a noteworthy fact that, contrary to the late winter fashions, the 'mixture of flowers and feathers are vetoed by .all the best milliners, who are seeking their inspirations from the prints of the early part of the last century, modifying the old shapes to suit the picturesque style of coiffure worn to-day. DAINTY CHILD'S FROCK. A charming example of a daintily pretty itut exceedingly simple child's frock is illustrated in our sketch. The material of which it is fashioned is a white Jap washing silk. costing, I believe, Is. qd. a .yard. Over the shoulders is arranged a deep pointed yoke of tucked silk, crosseci by deep mitred points of Valenciennes insertion. On to the lowest row of insertion is gathered a frill of silk' edged with Valenciennes lace, and inserted to match the yoke. Below the frill</p>
        <p>comes a short bodice of tucked silk, on to which the skirt of the little gown is gathered in succes- sive rows of gaugings which are carried up to a point in front. The skirt is full, is inserted to Match the yoke, and is finished with a full frill Lace edged and inserted like that upon the bodice. The sleeves, too, are full, the fulness being drawn into gaugings a little below the elbow and finished with lace-edged frills. A charming little bonnet of white crin, trimmed with daisies and white silk ribbon, Completes the pretty childish costume. THE HATS OF TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>In millinery, though the fact has hardly yet been driven home, says the Queen, the hats of to-day are so unlike the hats of yesterday that to , wear a last year's chapeau is to be utterly demode. Every hat to-day has a tilt—it may be • over the nose or over the right ear, but be tip- tiltetl it must. Those which are tipped up 'at the back over the forehead are, perhaps, the most modish, but they are not so easy to wear as the hat tilted to one side. Even the mushroom shape, which is still in evidence, inclines to one side, but it is much raised off the head by means of a bandeau. The bandeau is, indeed, the principal feature in hats just now. The majority run to extremes, either very largo or very small—there is no via media in millinery at present. Gigantic ostrich feathers fall from the backs of many of the smallest hats, and the pendent lace veil be- hind has still a following. Flowers are crushed into close-set wreaths surrounding the crowns and forming the bandeaux, and ril)bcms are much used in the shape of ruchings and looped sur- rounds to the crowns. It is obvious that the whole style of the bat depends entirely on the way it is set on the head, and the hair built up to fill in the brim. USEFUL CORSELET SKIRT.</p>
        <p>As the season advances the popularity of the corselet skirt seems to increase by leaps and bounds. I say corselet skirt advisedly, for it is a .skirt. unprovided with either bolero or cape, which promises to be the first favourite for summer wear with dainty, pretty blouses. Our sketch gives an excellent exorable of just such a useful corselet skirt for regular everyday wear. The material of which it is fashioned is a smooth</p>
        <p>rge, in colour a dark blue tfieugh not quite so deep in shade as navy. The skirt starts from a point 'about five inches above" the waist- line, fits accurately to the waist and hips, and falls thence in graceful folds to the hem. Over the shoulders come bretelles of the serge, covered with rows of machine stitching and . fastened to no skirt, back and front, with large handsome buttons. A band of machine stitching outlines the top of the skirt, and a similar band, four or fine inches in width, takes the place of ahem at the bottom.</p>
        <p>~.re".... -;.45.p. ,144,'• ' -!:',,ill'it.;," ~.) .°,'SV:I..S, ~ 0 • •15. , IX, i 1 1"-- • * ‘... . ' it)' .. : .i'.i '1 ;^:lii \ • I i, 9 \ •!- ~ _l* ' \ • 4 ' iiii..v..'. , i •• ' ' \‘),,, ' 41. li, V.' 'i ,tl, ,•:'.'•.2. • , I LT'.</p>
        <p>, , am.1.6.1.1••••••••••.,1.11,.\v,V,1:77,1:, ...... , -,- tiA7:lk , .i...i ..,:i 4 /::‘!//k:.:.''' / ' '&lt;:...411 A. \ *.,.'':.•; •ity \ ' ``V,.-"•-•.•L'` /1/ l r .11 ti:di ot k., I/ / • \:•'f.:'2..4:::?.`.**--,--. :-7-77-----rE.....144t-----:-..• - • fine se-</p>
