      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-05_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>DAINTINESS IN NECKWEAR.</head>
        <p>DAINTINESS IN NECKWEAR.</p>
        <p>• Fresh linen morning shirts have brought "eith them quite a new array of dainty neckwcar in I the shape of rabats, jabots, ties., bows, and collars. For all these effective accessories white lis, of course, the great favourite, while linon, batiste, and lace or embroidered linen are much preferred and far more chic than silk. Esqui- site little rabats of linon and Valenciennes, fur- ther adorned with hand-embroidered spots and openwork, can be acquired foe a few shillings, I while for the fastidious there are some delight- ful jabots of Irlande, real Valeneiennes, Bruges, &amp;c., worked with such taste and daintiness as to suggest the handiwork of fairy fingers. Em- broidery, though less effective than lace, is• also a very charming ornamentation, and the newest stiff linen collars all boast of some embroidered designs in white or colour. These collars are ever so much .more becoming to the face than stock collars, which are so difficult to hold in proper place.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-05_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>TO-DAY'S RECIPE.</head>
        <p>TO-DAY'S RECIPE.</p>
        <p>- EELS A LA TARTARE.—Skin and dear a good- e;7.cd eel, remove the head. Tie it round with the tail in the neck. . Place it in a saucepan with sufficient water to cover it, and half a glass of vinegar some salt, pepper, views, thyme, a bay leaf, Parsley, and onions cut in fine rings. Let these cook for twenty minutes over a slow fire. The water should only just simmer. Remove the eel, and let it get cold. When quite cold, untie' the string, make a batter with a little flour and the liquor the fish was cooked in, brush over the eel with this, roll in fine breadcrumbs , and karsley, salt and pepper. Beat up an egg with one spoonful of water and one of oil, .roll the SO3 in this and theu in breadcrurnbs again. Fry m very hot lard.</p>
        <p>/Fr.;t77</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-05_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>WORKS' COMPETITION FINAL.</head>
        <p>WORKS' COMPETITION FINAL.</p>
        <p>The final for the Warrington Works' Competition was played on the iI d erspo ol ground on Friday and, despite the ~xtremely inclement state of the weather attracted a fairly large crowd. Fletchers' kicked off against the wind and rain and Crosfield's at once assumed the aggressive, only to be met with a stubborn defence. Moreover they were rather slow at taking openings and it was nearly half time before they could score, Dunbobbin capping a passing movement amongst the backs by crosssng at the corner. In the second half the ground was almost like a quagmire and the players on neither side could do themselves justice. Fletcher's by the aid of the wind bad the best of the play. Croslield's stopped every attempt at scoring. It seemed, however, that Fletchers would pull the game out of the fire in the last minute or two of the game as two or three hot scrimmages took place under, the goal _posts, and the ball was almost grounded several times. .Eventually, however, Lawton, Crosfield's full hack, succeeded in relieving the ball safely dodn the field and the whistle sounded for the final. Crosfield's winning by a try to nil. After the game a rush was made for the grand stand from which the medals were presented to the winners and runners up. In performing this office, Alderman Tinnion, who spoke as one of the oldest members of the Warring- ton Football Club, said he regretted very much the wet night, as it had affected the" gate "and thus lessened the amount which would he available for local charities The Warrington club had taken the lead in competitions such as those, and he belie 7ed they were the only club which did set themselves benefit by the financial result of the competition. All was given to charities after the expenses had been paid. He was pleased to see the new style played during those matches. He was not in favour of tinkering with the laws of the Northern Union but he thought that the Warrington style was the best, and he believed that if it were adopted, it would do much to improve the play. He hoped that some change would be made in the forthcoming season, because it would add much to the enjoyment of the spectators. With regard to the games in the competi- tion, there had been 28 matches played and over £lOO had been taken. After paying all expenses, a sum of somewhere about £5O would be handed over to Warring- ton charities. (Applause). Amid cheers, the medals were then handed to the I members of the two teams. _ .</p>
        <p>Mr. J. E. Warren then presented the Warrington Junior League cup ad medals to the joint holders, Victoria Park Rangers and Orford Hornets. In doing so, he said in these degenerate days of Rugby football, when there was such a suicidal division in the Rugby camp, it behoved every club to put forth its whole strength to help the junior clubs. The Warrington club had done its best in this matter, but he would be glad if the members of the League would do their best also to fulfil their fixtures, and thus let the League be carried on smoothly. A League was of much more value than a competition carried on on the lines of the Works competition, because it formed a training ground for young players, who, if they showed good form, would be drafted into the town team. It might seem selfish on the part of the club, but he took it that every player who took part in the game did his utmost to get as high as he could. He hoped that the club which did so much for junior football would be well supported. (Applause.) The presentations were then made by Mr. Warren. The proceedings were brought to a close by a hearty vote of thanks accorded to Alderman Tinnion and Mr. Warren on the motion of Mr.H. Ashton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-05_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>BURGLARY AT MOORE, During Saturday night the residence of Mr. Ernest Edward Ridgway, of Moore was burglariously entered and several articles were carried off.—The affair was not discovered until Sunday morning, when the house- hold rose to find one of the windows open. On look- ing round several things were noticed out of place, and it was eventually found that two silver cigarette cases, a silver tobacco box.a silver snuff box,a silver sovereign purse, a silver photo frame and a silver tray were missing.—At the Warrington Occasional Court on Tuesday afternoom, a middle-aged man named William Atkinson was charged with breaking into Mr. Ridgway's house and stealing the articles named.—Mr. Ridgway related the facts, and identified two or three of the missing articles which have been recovered.— P.C. Preston spoke to receiving prisoner into custody from the Salford police that morning, and in reply to the charge he said, " That is all."—P.S. Barlow asked for a remand to the Daresbury Sessions on Tuesday next, which Mr. F. A. Frost (the magistrate) granted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-05_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>*THEFT OP CIGARETTES.</head>
        <p>*THEFT OP CIGARETTES.</p>
        <p>- At Warrington on Tuesday Mabel Rahan, a native of London, IN'LLS charged with stealing a box of cigarettes. value 45., from the shop of Mr. Albert Taylor, tobacconist, 18, Buttermarket-street, on Monday night.—Superin- tendent Monaghan stated that at 8.40 prisoner was seen by P.C. Andrews loitering about. Eventually she went into the shop and reached a box of cigarettes from a shelf behind the counter. She put it under her cape and came into the street again. Prosecutor's wife noticed her take something and leave the shop hurriedly. She followed prisoner and asked her what she had got and she replied "only a box" P.C. Andrews brought her to the Police Station, where she admitted stealing the cigarettes. She was under the influence of drink at the time, and had pawned her husband's trousers and vest in order to get liquor. She had only been in War- rington a month, and had been lodging in Ship-yard and Friars Green.—Prisoner pleaded for leniency, and said she was under the influence of drink and did not know what she was doing.—She was fined 10s. including costs, or seven days imprisonment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-05_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church. At this Church on Sunday morning 'qr. E. F. Clarke preached on the words "They waited for him." In the evenihg the pulpit was occupied by the Rev. John Gunnell, who based a powerful sermon on the account of the healing of the Syrophenician woman's daughter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-05_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>Bold-strekt Wesleyan Church.</head>
        <p>Bold-strekt Wesleyan Church.</p>
        <p>At this Church on Sunday mornir'p, the preacher MS the Rev. W. Mellor who based his discourse on the words :—" Are they not all ministering spirits ?" The evening service took the form of a raemorial service to Wesleyan workers who have recently passed away. The Rev. J.Alanning preached, his subject being : "Hope beyond the grave." He made sympathetic allusion to the death of Mr. Alfred Larnplugh who formerly resided in Warrington and was a very active and devoted Wesleyan, having held most of the offices open to lay- men, and also referred to the loss which the choir had sustained by the death of Miss E. ounn, a highly esteemed member who died after a very short Mims*. At the conclusion of the service Miss Sterling, who presided at the organ played : "0 rest in the Lord," the congregation standing as a mark of respect to the departed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-05_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>Sunday School Teacher's Association.</head>
        <p>Sunday School Teacher's Association.</p>
        <p>The quarterly meeting of this Association was held at Fairfield Schools on Monday evening, the Rector (Rev. Canon Willis) presiding over a good attendance. An interesting address on " Religious instruction of the young" was delivered by the Bev. G. B. Thurston, Rector of Lymm who alluded to the church's care for her children through the various stages of their lives. He touched briefly upon the Education Bill which, be said, had aroused their indignation end specially con- demned the clause which made attendance at religious instruction entirely optional. An interesting discussion followed, Mr. Thurston being cordially thanked for his address. Light refreshments were afterwards served.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-05_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>Wycliffe Sunday Mani Anniversary.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe Sunday Mani Anniversary.</p>
        <p>The anniversary services in connection with Wycliffe Sunday school were held on Sunday and throughout the \day were largely attended. The preacher in the morn- ing and evening was the Rev. Professor Armitage, of the United College, Bradford, who in the evening spoke from the words : " And his sons went and feasted in I their houses, every one his day, and sent and called their three sisters to eat and drink with them, and it was so, I when:lth° days of their feasting were gone about that Job sent and sanctified them, and rose up early in the morning and offered burnt offerings according to the number of them all ; for Job said, It may be that my sons have sinned and renounced God in their hearts. Thus did Job continually." Addressing a large congre- gation, he said the text presented a picture of home life ,at a time when the father was the priest of the family to whom it lay to offer sacrifices and to lead the domestic worship, a priesthood \of unchanging and Divine ordination. Many fathers and mothers were anxious' that their chil- dren should grow up nicely that they should be well dressed, well spoken, and well behaved but the deeper , things did not come into their hearts. There was no going into the chamber and shutting the door and of uplifting the hands in intercession and prayer. He pleaded that night with all who had been placed at the head of a family concerning their solemn vocation. In the story of Job they had presented the picture of the boys and girls growing up' in the privileges of manhood and womanhood and Job the father watching them aexiously, and interceding on their behalf. He thanked God that they all believed in intercession. Their belief was not reached through argument or scientific reasoning but through the instincts of the spirit. Philosophy was foolishness when they came to the deep things of the spirit. They were seeking in that church the npbuilding of spiritual life, but he would be making a great mistake who sought in spiritual selfishness to obtain private and personal perfection. There might be a certain beauty in such endeavours but the real tasutywould come as they fulfilled the Divine purpose for which they were put into a home. They belonged to a body which con- demned narrow sacerclotalism in order that they might embrace the broader sacerdotalism that all men were ' priests. They were all priests. They were all the people of God sent to b 3 prophets and priests, to speak the Word and to hold sacrifice and cupplication, and ' upon every man and woman, every father and. mother, that duty devolved and that great opportunity opened. In the morning and the evening the choir ably rendered special anthems, and the music throughout was of an appropriate character. In the aft:Tnoon a joint service was held for the school children and the P.S.A., at which there was a crowded attendance. Mr. George Brown presided, and said that to many present the happiest days of their I i Yes were those which they spent in the Wycliffe Sunday school,and if they maintained ther interest and sympathy in the work of the school their youthful days would be renewed. He appealed to parents to show a desire that their children should attend thekSunday school. If they did that and the children learnt that their fathers and mothers took an interest in what was going ow in the Sunday school, they would be encouraged, and would themselves be interested in the school. (Hear, hoar). An interesting address was delivered by Mrs. C. E. r Parker upon the subject contained in the words "As a man soweth so shall he also reap." If the children sowed in falsehood they could only look for mistrust and want of confidence but if they scattered seeds of kindness and of truth they would reap a harvest of joy and gladnr -a. Youth was the time for sowing and the children should see that they planted when young those seeds which would give the best results. Many teachers were disappointed and discouraged because there was in the children a lack of sympathy, but they must not be so but must remember that when sown aseed had to remain hidden, and that there was no higher or nobler work than the training in goodness of young people.— During the proceedings special hymns were sang by the children, the efforts of the infants being particularly pleasing. A solo was taken by Mr. NV. J. Barnes and a duet by Affss Beswick and Miss Armstrong. The col- lections amounted to £lO.</p>
        <p>&amp;I'Mr. William H. Elgar, father of Sir Edward Blear, the famous musician, has died at Worcester, aged 84. The Bishop of Birmingham on Tuesday said the Church had for years educated the children of the work- ing class's, but the sentiments of that class had little in common with the Church. That was because the attitude of the Church had Always been a patronising one. It should really have its forudation in the labour- ug class.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-05_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>WISE DELAY.</head>
        <p>A WISE DELAY.</p>
        <p>NOW that most of our New Goods aye to hand, the thinking</p>
        <p>, man who did not get a Suit at Easter will find that</p>
        <p>the just-arrived ranges of Cloths in our windows will fully</p>
        <p>repay him for the delay.</p>
        <p>TO him, it will not ,be so much a matter fof expense as of choice, these tasteful and reGeed relections being very knoderetely priced, while the Designs and</p>
        <p>Colourings tt T' 0 most varied.</p>
