        <p>EYE-CATCHING POSTERS, ALL SIZES " EXAMINER " OFFICE, WARRINGTON. PRIMERS. BOOKBINDERS. LITHOGRAPTIERS. PAPER-RUMS. LITHOGRAPHER. PAPER-RULERS. TRADE PRINTING of every description. T“-rp EE ..USAMINER" PRINTING OFFICE REMY ETRIIRT, WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>The slenderness of the MR CHAMBERLAIN resources of statesman- AND THE BUDGET, ship that are now to be found among the Con- servative opposition in the House of Commons has been quite strikingly illustrated during the dis- cussions on Mr. Asnurrn's Budget. Mr. BALFOUR. was admittedly altogether at sea when his own CHANCELLOR OF THE EXCHEQUER being absent, he rose to follow Mr. ASQUITH on Monday. He could say nothing about the tobacco duties ; he con- fessed he made a rather feeble attempt to keep open the pathways to Protection; but the only matter with which, either on Monday or Tuesday, he felt himself able to cope with any confidence was the excellent intention of the Government to pay current expenditure out of the revenue of the year. According to Mr BALFOUR, that way ruin lies. As the opposite contention is the ons most easily sustained, the ex-Premier is at least entitled to the praise of being courageous, and of also diaplaying some ingenuity in argument. But as criticism of the Budget, his views may be put aside as not being germane to the matter. There are many things that Mr. BALFOUR does not understand, but he understands the intricacies of national finance least of all.</p>
        <p>It is exceedingly unfortunate that nobody now sits behind him who can hide the deficiencies of his party in that respect. Where is Mr. Glnsoxf Bowiss ? Where is Sir EDGAR VINCENT ?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-05_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="2" unit="words">
