      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-05_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>0 pay _ BU Db. /UMW. I "Where," as Sir Fadorms VILLON inquired so long FRMILY Givarr. – WIDNOB, ago, " are the snows of yesteryear ? " Mr. 13,41.14firft;</head>
        <p>0 pay _ BU Db. /UMW. I "Where," as Sir Fadorms VILLON inquired so long FRMILY Givarr. - WIDNOB, ago, " are the snows of yesteryear ? " Mr. 13,41.14firft;</p>
        <p>as we said, has no capable financier sitting behind him. He is still more unfortunate in the fact that the best speaker sitting beside him is concerned more with what this or that section of the House of Commons or the British people may think of his views and of his character than about any great questions of statesmanship. Mr. CHAMBERLAIN confined himself almost entirely to defending the family and the family cause. His son is absent from the House in consequence of a troablesome, though happily not a painful, illness, and so the father defended the estimates which it would have been the son's duty to defend if he had been present, but with an effrontery that the son, it may safely be pre- sumed, would not have had the daring to put on. The main point wasthat the Goiernment had b3en in power over a period of four months, that if it had cared to alter the Estimates it might have done so, and that, as it had taken them over pretty much as they were, it must have approved of them, whilst blaming those by whom they had been drawn up.. On that matter the reply was brief, sufficient, and cmclusive. A Cusbrcsrzoa or THE EXCHEQUER cannot construct a Budget in the midst of a General Election, but even if he could do so he cannot be responsible in February for the Estimates or the Apenditure of the previous September.</p>
        <p>Mr. CHAMBERLAIN'S speech was a striking revelation, not only of the weakness of the late self-glorifying Government in one of the most essential parts of statesmanship, but of Mr. CHAMBERLAIN'S own regard to veracity. His statement that when Lord LANSDOWNE left the Foreign Office "there was not a cloud on the horizon," may be left to speak for itself. Every- body knows that, as Mr. Asounn pointed out, Lord LANSDOWNE left behind him the gravest European situation that has been known for many years past—a situation that, as the CHANCELLOR. «,f the EXCHEQUER also remarked, has been cleared up and straightened out mainly by the tact and the ability of Sir EDWARD GREY. The real character of Mr. CnAmBERLAIN's speech is more aptly illus- trated by what he said on the subject of old age pensions. He had never, be declared, promised or been in favour of old age pensions. A general system of that kind was unpracticable, and would be immoral and wasteful, and so on. Mr. CHAMBERLAIN cannot know how greatly for a long time past his countrymen have been wondering at his most amazing audacity, nor can he have yet brought himself to understand that they are made indignant by the effrontery of such speeches, which are, indeed, an insult to even the meanest intelligence. '</p>
        <p>After all there is a fair</p>
        <p>WAttniNerroisi posssibility that Warrington DOCK. may some day have a dock of its own on the Ship Canal. The day has gone by for the Ship Canal Company to undertake the work, and the county borough will therefore be dependent on its own enterprise in the matter. Fortunately, the Ship Canal Company is favourably disposed to War- rington entering upon some such enterprise and has agreed not to tip spoil any nearer the old river bed than is necessary. According to Alderman Bennett—who is showing a zeal in the matter all the more praiseworthy in that it is excep- tional—Arpley meadows will be covered over with mountains of mud in four or five years unless the Corporation hastens to "stake out claims for posterity." We were</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-05_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="2" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-05_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <p>very pleased to note. that the Alderman's lengthy speech on the dock question, and his criticism of 'Corporation apathy, were listened to with atten- tion. He often has it urged against him that he is a dreamer, but there is one goad thing about AfF. Bennett's dreams—they look forward to things which are seldom impracticable, even if inexpedient. It is too much to expect the Cor- poration to go in for a dock scheme straight away, but they might atleast take Mr. Bannett's advice and prevent the natural advantages of the site being obliterated by spoil dredged from the river. There is already the nucleus of a dock in the growino lay-by at Walton—and here again the Ship Cant;l Company are sympathetically inclined. It is obviously the bounden duty of the Corporation to keep itself au fait with the dock 'question and not lose sight of such opportunities of bringing boundless prosperity to the town as 'flay quite conceivably arise.</p>
        <p>---- Councillor Pemberton on AN IMPORTANT T u esd a y broke down PRINCIPLE. another of those barriers of secrecy which have done so much to envelop the Warrington Corporation in I . an atmosphere of public distrust in recent years ' He carried an important point when he got the Council to adopt the following resolution regard- ing the purchase of property: "That no property ,4 be purchased by any official or committee of this ' Corporation without the sanction of the Council, and that all minutes relating to the purchase of property by any committee shall be printed on the Cmincil agenda." Corporations have a way ofishrouding all qnestions relating to property-buying in the deepest secrecy. It is a sort of tradition with them that all anzling in the waters of the property market must be done in the dark—else the fish won't bite, or if they do, someone is sure to see and cast a line themselves. Doubtless, considerable circum- spection is necessary in these matters. It would not do for the Town Clerk to go on to the house-tops and announce the requirements of the 'Corporation to all and sundry. Councillor Pemberton has no desire to let light into negoti- ations which may be of a delicate character, but he is justified in insisting on the ordinary mem- ber's right to know when the Corporation has got "the refusal" of s contemplated investment in land or property, and the further right of express- ing his opinion for or against the prospective purchase. Important questions of public policy may easily be affected by such a purchase, and it should not be left to any official or small corn rnittee to close a bargain unknown to the Council " a whole. We rejoice that the principle of the lzotion was so readily accepted. ----</p>
        <p>--- The time has now come for Tlra INFIRMARY an appeal to the generosity EXTENSION. of the public to enable the Governors of .the Infirmary , t° carry out the scheme of extension recently resolved upon. The response, we trust, will be 1 'Worthy of the town and of the extremely deserving ] rleture of the object. Funds could not he asked , for in a better cause. The work of the Infir- Mary needs very little commendation on ' our part. It is well known and highly ePPreciated by all sections of the community, and reore particularly by the working-classes to whom the institution is an inestimable boon in times of sickness and accident. That an extension should : be undertaken—and at an early date—is impera- tive if the Infirmary is to realize its fullest Measure of usefulness. Of late years there has, been an enormous increase in the nurober of , oases treated, and this has naturally involved ' a great strain upon the resources and accorn- , A leodation of the institution. Unfortunately " 't has occasionally necessitated the discharge of Patients sooner than desirable, while on the other hand Niles have been turned away chiefly °wing, to the lack of room. The fact should be a,eriouslv taken to heart that Warrington has less . 14,fixtnar-y accommodation per head of population "flan any of the large towns in the country,having , ;14 average of only .59 beds per thousand of popu- iltion,as compared with I.3,the aver'age of 30 towns °E a somewhat similar character. The proposed , eXtension, which will increase the accommodation to a total of 100 beds, will cost £15,000. • •!°- Preliminary list of donations, onblished ,• iercln anaopthpeorai column, amounts to £3,335 ss. ' balance of £ll6 now going forth that the .664 15s. may Wartangtonians soon be raised. It will require a bigweffillort but weleel confident that rise to the occasion,' deter- mined et er_ i ~. mined that their infirmary shall in size as 1, already is in efficiency, be thoroughly worthy of a great industrial community. The Warrington Rural Ps , MORE ABOUT DUST. tract Connell will probably are long, endeavour to stir "./ up the Rural District Comacils' Association with , _regard to the dust nuisance on the highways. Mr. Brooks, the chairman, spoke at some length en that subject at the Board's meeting on Wed- nesday and suggested that it should be fully discussed at a future meeting. Re expressed the Opinion that for every mile of country highway there was enough land put out of cultivation by the dust to make a decent-sized farm. This may be a slight exaggeration if taken literally, hut it represents the views of the farming classes. Some- thing will certainly have to ne done,and tho sooner ,the Rural Councils' Association and higher authori- ties seriously, begin to'consider ways and means and 1 the better. The remedy seems to be in road-con- su c t i o n , and we note that the rural district ,t Bnrveyor,Mr. Newcombe, suggests tarred macadam es the basis. There is no doubt that heavier and /Pore binding materials are a sine qua non. It is equally certain that the cost should not wholly fall c'll either the rural authorities or the motorists Perhaps the problem will result in the nationalisa- tion of main roads receiving more attention from . the legislature. It would he a very good way of centralising the responsibility and leaving the , question of 2. s. d. in safe hands. --</p>
        <p>-- • When Mr. John Burns was PRETTY PENSIONS. appointed President of the Local Government Board it Was asked whether he would receive a pension on • retiring from office. It was pointed out in reply that that was impossible, AS all the pensons avail- ' able were already appropriated. On Wednesday air. McKenna gave the following list of Ex-Cabinet ' 4nisters in receipt of pensions : Lord George Hamilton £2,000 . Lord Cross 2,000 'Mr. Chaplin 1,200 Sir John Gorst Lord Balfour of Burleigh , Mr. Gerald Balfour It will he observad that all the pensions are at resent monopolised by members of that political I:arty whose supporters are not generally supposed he the most necessitous. But special interest 7ill attach to the particular circumstances which, the opinion of the late Government, entitled Tr. Burns' immediate predecessor at the Local government Board, Mr. Gerald Balfour, to this c:lndsome recognition. Then too, there is Lord ess, who ought to he a valuable national IhiTBet. twit is not. He has been drawing, ,18 2,000 a year for we don't know how long— entv or twenty-five years. The services are Iladeed rated high of the man who receives a gift ii(sl £40,000 or £.50,000 from his employers after „18 Work is done and paid for. And yet old-age a",e,nsions for the humble are said to be an impos- R")le dream in the present state of national '4lance !</p>
        <p>Chamber of Commerce. meeting of the Warrington Chamber of Com- eroe was held on Monday, with the President 'onncillor Fulton !Smith) in the elm&amp; Alderman 'ftnett (secretary) submitted the report of the dele- Rotes to the annual meeting of Associated Chambers Commerce, the Conference on Municipal Accounts, tu) d the Meeting of Secretaries. Mr. Holman Kingdon „Ilbraitted valuable details of a scheme for the further 'ft endment of the patent law. The secretary also 6teeented a report re his interview with the Ship &lt;, .4%114 Company on the Warrington Dock question. „Vhilst declining," he said, "to enter into any .irnite engagement, the Canal Company' had expressed tbeir Willingness to co-operate with the Corporation for V provision of suitalile facilities for dealing with tieZttrington goods, and to favourably consider any, tl IWOnita suggestion in reference to the construction of (</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-05_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>The destruction of the great Californian city promise s to be the making of a record year in the steel industry of the world.--" Times."</p>
        <p>* * * An absent-minded woman the other day.went to the bank to cash a cheque her husband had sent her. She endorsed it thus: Your loving wife, Mary Miller.</p>
        <p>* *- A Frodsham correspondent writes :—The cuckoo was heard here for the first time on Tuesday morning on the Overton Hills. We may add that it was heard at Latchford on Monday morning. * * *</p>
        <p>It takes years of residence in London (says a New York critic) to find out that " Cherry 'ouse " stands for Charing Cross, and " Skenzen " means South Kensington, and that " Emsmeth " is the proper pro- nunciation (underground) of Hammersmith. * * *</p>
        <p>When the Great Sankey Parish Council agreed to support the provision of a police station in the village, Mr. W. Furness thought it necessary to dispel any impression that the lawlessness of the inhabitants demanded the extra accommodation. The new build- ing, he said, was not required for the residents but only for undesirables who visited the locality. * * *</p>
        <p>Some more picturesque phraseology NM employed by Councillor Chantler at Tuesday's Council meeting in describing the condition of certain streets in Howley Ward. He said a visitor to the neighbourhood would think there had been an eruption-Jin fact, the Council was led to suppose that, -Vesuviusl wasn't in it with Howley. * * *</p>
        <p>The rigour of the regulations regarding free travelling on the tramcars has been relaxed to the extent that tramway-men and policemen may travel free when in uniform There was absolutely no sense in making a rule to the contrary, and we are glad to see that the committee in its calm movements has seen the wisdorn of making an exception to he rule. * * *</p>
        <p>There is no reply yet from Mr. Carnegie to War- ringtm's application for a library donation. The man of millions may have gat an inkling that the people of Warrington are for from unanimous in desiring his money. Meanwhile Bl ickpool has pocketed its pride and is gloating over no less a sum than R,15,000 which it has obtained from the golden hand of the Pittsburg ironmaster. * *</p>
        <p>Mr. A. 1-1. Crosfield, M.P., wired us yesterday from Pen, a suburb of Constantinople, to say that he expected to arrive at Westminster on Monday evening. The telegram was handed in at Pera at a quarter to nine; in the morning, and after a race with Father Time reached Warrington at 8.26 a.m., thus gaining 19 minutes on the journey. It is a pity man cannot travel as fast as his messages. If he could, instead of the well-worn adage about Time waiting for no man, the position would be reversed. * * * - -</p>
        <p>Many of our readers will be much relieved to learn from the paragraph in our local news column that Mr. Henry Whithatn and his family are safe in San Francisco. Mr. Whitham reports that his home is "pretty well wrecked," a phrase which is suggestive of the pluck and optimism of the writer He is obviously undismayed, even at the age of 70, and his many friends and relatives will, we are sure, join with 119 in wishing him good fortune in the reconstruction of hearth and home. *s*</p>
        <p>Macclesfield Town Council has refused by 20 votes to 18 votes to issue Risters and handbills calling attention to the " abuse of alcohol." The " trade ' raised so strong an agitation that they blocked the motion, thus being more successful than they were in Warrington. One Councillor quoted Gladstone's des- cription of beer as " a divine drink," and Napoleon's declaration that it was no use to endeavour to invade England whilst the British drank beer. It is a pity, however, that the Council could not see that all this was no argument against teaching the townspeople the danger of drinking to excess. * * *</p>
        <p>Cheery optimism is one of the characteristics of the Beautiful Warrington Society enthusiasts. Apart frbm the leading supporters of the movement, there was only an attendance of about half a dozen at the St. John's and Orford joint ward meeting on Monday evening. The speakers, however, consoled themselves with the reflection that " great things have small beginnings!" and the enthusiastic manner in which the work has been taken up gives hope that even these wards will in time be clothed in beauty. .It requires st..etch of the imagination to conjure up such a picture—especially in the Case of St. Johns' Ward.</p>
        <p>* *</p>
        <p>The following, which we cull from " The Tatler," illustrates one of the best samples of unintentional humour we have come across for a long time :—I am told there is a beggtr in the West-end who, posing as an old soldier, has a board hung round his neck with the following notice : BLIND TIIII3T7GII AN ACCIDENT. Engagements 7 Wounds Children 6</p>
        <p>Total 22 ***</p>
        <p>There were two very well-known and able barristers engaged in the case against Councillor Peacock in the persons of Mr. Rigby Swift and Mr. W. Madden. Both these gentlemen practise largely in Liverpool and probably have a considerable respect for one another. This fact, does not, however prevent them indulging in occasional barristerial bickerings about points of etiquette and order. There were several of these passages-at-arms in which one turned to the other— they sat together—and metaphorically pulverized him with cutting sarcasm. We should think the score was about even at the close of hostilities, which were always a source of amusement to the Court. * * *</p>
        <p>Those people who attended the Police Court on Thursday for the hearing of a charge of corrupt prac- tice against a member of the Town Council had plenty of opportunity for railing against the cumbrous character of the legal machinery. The case might have been over before dinner time but for the fact that the practice of taking down the evidence in long- hand still obtains. Quarter Sessions and Assize cases thus drag themselves out to an inordinate length, and in the end the evidence is not so well and accurately transcribed as it would be from a shorthand note which might be taken in a quarter of the time, and read over te the witness afterwards. The latter practice obtains in Courts-martial, and we cannot see any reason why it should not be adopted in the criminal courts. The law seems to love to bind itself up with formalities and red-tape. After acting as a kind of shuttlecock between the Education Committee and the Town Council, Mr. A.J. Jolley's election to the former body has been at last confirmed. The great objection to*Mr. Jolley was on the ground that he is not an economist and that he was rejected by the ratepayers in a municipal con- test. We are prepared to admit that Mr. Jolley has notions of efficiency which many people have a perfect right to deem extravagant, but as Councillor Stott pointed out, the Education Committee consists of over 30 members and they are not likely to be led into needless expenditure solely by Mr. Jolley. There is no doubt of his abilities as an educationist, fortun- ately, and we think that his value to the committee as an expert outweights the objections to him on the score of economy. 'The Council was evidently of the same mind when it confirmed Mr. Jolley's election by 10 votes to 10.'</p>
        <p>Trades and Labour Council Visit the Museum' At the invitation of Mr. C. Madeley, curator and I lrbrarian, thirty delegates of the Warrington Trades and Labour Council visited the Museum on Wednes- day evening. Alderman Bennett, chairman of ,the ' Museum Committee, was also present and offered a ' welcome to the visitors. The various departments of the institution were inspected and Mr. Madoley gave exhaustive explanations of the exhibits. The party had also the opportunity of seeing the books of the new school library which had just been prepared for issue to the elementary schools. A hearty vote of thanks was accorded to Mr. Madeley on the motion of Mr. Plumb, president of the Trades Council, seconded by Mr. Bullock and supported by Councillor Stott (secretary), and Mr. Rendell. Ur. Madeley in acknowledging the compliment expressed the hope that the result of the Council's visit would be that a greater interest would be taken in the Museum by the members of the Council and the working-classes generally.</p>
        <p>, • -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-05_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>INTERESTING MARRIAGE. Considerable interest was taken both in Runcorn and St. Helens in the marriage which was solemnised in the Parish Church, St. Helens last Thursday afternoon. The contracting parties were Miss Charlotte Netta Poole, alder daughter of Mr. and Mrs. J. Poole, of Linghohne terrace. St. Helens, and formerly of Runcorn, and Mr. Harold °ramrod Cowper, second son of the late Mr. Cowper,of Accrington. Mr. Cowper is at the St. Helens- branch of the Manchester and Counties Bank Limited. Thei bride was charmingly attired in ivory crepe de chene over taffeta silk, and white hat to match, trimmed with tulle and hyacinths. She also wore a pearl and turquoise brooch, which, with a charming bouquet of lilies of the valley and roses, which she carried, were the gifts of the bridegroom. The brides- maid was Miss Daisy Poole, whose dress was of beaver coloured taffetas with facings of goblin blue and cream lace, and hat trimmed with goblin blue. The bridegroom had presented to her a bouquet of pink tulips and gold curb bangle. Mrs. Poole was gowned in black crepe-de-lhene, with heliotrope toque, and bouquet of parma violets and hyacinths. The bride was given away by her father. Mr. R. B. Woodward presided at the organ and played appropriate music during the ceremony. The wedding party after- wards drove to the residence of the bride's parents, where luncheon was served and, later in the afternoon the happy couple left for Derhyshire,for the honeymoon</p>
        <p>RUN CORN WOMAN'S BAD RECORD. Elizabeth Barker, a Runcorn woman, with a bad record, was brought up at the Police Court last week, before Messrs. E. liaison and D. Bisbrown,charged with having been drunk and disorderly on the night previous. —Sergeant Wynne proved the charge' and said prisoner raised the whole neighbourhood by her filthy and dis- gusting language.—Superintendent Ennion said there were sixty-five previous convictions. On the last occasion she was committed to prison for seven months, in default of finding sureties. Since then she had been before the Court for vagrancy, but was discharged on undertaking to go to the Workhouse.—Prisoner said she left the Workhouse because she did not see that it was right for her to impose on the ratepayers so long as she was able to work. She only remained at Dutton two days.—Mr. Imison said see did not appear to have any anxiety respecting the ratepayers when sent to prison. The cost was just as great.—Upon prisoner giving a promise "to do her best " to reform, she was discharged.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-05_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS. FortnigiWieeting.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS. FortnigiWieeting.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Board of Guardians was hell at Dutton on Tuestlay when there were present : Messrs. A. it. Norman (presiding), A. H. Talbot, S. Woodward,T. Wright, J. B. Chadwick, W. M. Morris, J. Whitley, J. Spruce, J. Hallows, H. Eyes, W. Harrison. S. faylor, G. Pilling, A. Broome, G. Egerton, Warburton, P. Collins, J. W. Temperley, J. H. Davies, T. Antwis, H. M. Davies, E. Bretherton, S. F. Ashton (clerk), W. E. Hough (assistant clerk), dePrez, Pearson and Cook (relieving officers) and J. Cunnah (workhouse masters). MAINTENANCR OF LUNA TICS. The Clerk intimated that the rate of maintenance of pauper lunatics at the County Asylum for the quarter ending June 30th, had been fixed at 88. TREATMENT OF VAGRANT CHILDREN. The Board were asked by the St. Giles Union to request Ulla local members of Parliament to present a petition against the " Bill to provide for the better protection of children of vagrant parents," the import- ance of which on account of its brevity, was likely to be overlooked. ; The Clerk pointed out that a Committee had been appointed to attend a Conference on vagrancy to beheld on Friday. It was decided to postpone any action until after the Conference gathering.</p>
        <p>FINANCIAL</p>
        <p>..... The Finance Committee reported the payment in out- relief of £7O to Mr. Cook, £26 to Mr. De Pre z, and £25 to Mr. Pearson. The total payments were £179 4g. 4d.' and there was a balance in hand of £5,263 is. id.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-05_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL. The Council and Motor Traffic.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL. The Council and Motor Traffic.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Rural Dis- trict Council was held at Dutton on Tuesday when there were present—Messrs. G.Pilling (presiding),A.H. Talbot, S. Woodward, T. Wright, J. B. Chadwick, J. Whitley, J. Spruce, J. Hallows, H. Eyes, W. Harrison, A. Broome, G. Egerton Warburton, J. H. Davies, T. Antwis, H. M. Davies, E. Eretherton, G. F. Ashton (clerk), W. E. Hough (assistant clerk), W. Diggle and J. Swinton (surveyors), J. Farrington (inspector), and Dr. Adams (medical oiticer).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-05_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>MOTOR NUISANCE..</head>
        <p>THE MOTOR NUISANCE..</p>
        <p>The St. Albans Rural Council forwarded the follow- ingiresolution for approval: "that in the opinion of this Council the serious attention of H.A. Government should be drawn to the terrible nuisance and damage caused by motor vehicles in this district ; that this Council is of opinion that all motor vehicles should be in some measure further taxed and that a grant from the revenue so derived should be made towards the expense of watering the roads in every. district used by motor vehicles in transit to other districts with a view to reducing the vexatious and damaging nuisance caused by the dust, the aforesaid damage and nuisance particularly affecting the farmers' crops for a considerable distance from such roads and also other property immediately adjoining such roads; that this Council is of opinion that the cost of prevent- ing damage so caused should not be borne by the local rates, but by the owners of the motor vehicles ; further that serious danger accrued to persons using the high- ways from motor vehicles raising such great clouds of dust as renders the sight of other vehicles almost impos- sible to give time to got out of the way." Mr. Woodward moved that the resolution be allowed to lie on the table as the Council had expressed their opinion before. The opinion was synonymous with the opinion contained in the resolution, and had been expressed at a meeting called by the Council. Mr. A. G. Guest seconded. Mr. W. Harrison moved and Mr. Antwis seconded the supporting of the resolution, and upon a vote being taken, it was decided to take the latter course.</p>
        <p>TREE PLANTING AT STOCKTON HEATH. The Clerk reported the receipt of a letter from teh Clerk to the Stockton Heath Parish Council enclosign a letter from Mr. A. Fawcett, hon. secretary of the 'tree Planting Committee, asking whether the Council would be prepared to take over the 325 trees in the parish or whether they would allow the Parish Council to take them over. The committee gave an undertaking to maintain and look after the trees for five years. The Chairman explained that the Parish Council of Latchford had taken the trees over. Mr. Hallows moved that the Parish Council be allowed to take over the trees. Mr. Antwis moved that the matter be adjourned. It vrq rather an important question and ought to be freely ventilated. Mr. Piling pointed out that there was nothing con- tentious in the motion. It was merely a question as to whether the Parish Council should take over the trees or not. Mr. Halton's motion was approved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-05_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>A ST. HELENS COMPANY. Mr. Justice Buckley heard an aeplication in the High Court on Tuesday, that the petition of E. A. Magnifico for the winding up of the St. Helens Smelting Com- pany, Limited, might be withdrawn, on the ground that the creditors' debt had been discharged. but his lord- ship preferred to dispose of the case by dismissing the petition. Judgment accordingly.</p>
        <p>- - KILLED BY A FALL OF ROOF. An inquest was held at Garswood, near St. Helens, on Tuesday, on the body of Henry Fairclough (31), a colliery drawer, who was killed by a fall of roof in the Winstanley Colliery on Saturday last. The accident occurred after a shot had been fired, though the roof was considered safe. A verdict of " Accidental death" was returned.</p>
        <p>„ ST. HELEN'S BOY'S DEATH. Henry Fildes, aged nine, of 32, York-steet, St. Helens, died late on Sunday night in the hospital from injuries , believed to have been caused by failing from a wall onl Saturday. Mr. Jones held the inquest at Peasleyl Cross on Monday, when Peter Huyton, aged nine, the boy's companion, said deceased was trying to walk along a ledge by the side of the house in order to get to the footbridge in Albion-street to go over the brook, when he fell into the bed of the brook, a distance of about ten feet. He cut his chin. Witness pulled him out of the water and tie went home crying.—The mother said that Dr. Giles attended the lad and had him seat to the hospital, where he died from shock and exhaustion. —A verdict of ” Accidental death " was returned. DESERTED FOR NINETEEN YEARS. John Greig, a bottlemaker, was remanded at St. Helens, on Monday, in order that he might pay the cost or'a constable's journey to Glasgow to arrest him. Greig's wife said her husbancl had been away from her nineteen years. He left her with a young baby, and she had had to work and bring up a family without any help from her husband. She got an order for Is. per week in 1892, but he never paid her a penny. Some time ago he came to St. Helens and saw his wife. She had him arrested, but forgave him all the arrears on his promise that he would keep up the payments in future. He went back to Scotland, leaving word that he had gone to South Wales. After some diliiculty Greig's whereabouts were found. That morning a money order , was received for the arrears. Greig said he bad nothing ' with which to pay the expenses. A SABBATH NUISANCE. - - •</p>
        <p>' Five young men, living in the neighbourhood of Park- road and Blackbrook, were charged at St. Helens on Monday with being part of a gang of Sunday gamblers. —John Thomas Lloyd and James Dolan were charged with gambling, and Charles Stewart, Sam Pennington, and Peter Gilligan were charged with aiding and abetting.—Police-constables Tunstall and Merrill said they found about forty men gambling in Leyland-street, at 3.30 p.m. They went back to the Town Hall and got Sergeant Moffat and live constables to help them to surround the men. As they were approaching Stewart, who was standing on the new railway, gave the signal and the men scattered. Five of them were arrested.— Lloyd and Dolan, with Stewart, were fined Os. each or seven days, and the other two were fined Gs. 6d. or seven days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-05_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>CRIPPLED GIRLS' FLORAL EXHIBITION. _ Pretty :Scalia at the Parr Hall.</head>
        <p>CRIPPLED GIRLS' FLORAL EXHIBITION. _ Pretty :Scalia at the Parr Hall.</p>
        <p>The Parr Hall is this week bright with the choicest blooms of every tint. The beautiful rose, the rich chrysanthemum, the delicate pansy—in fact specimens of all the best flowers which grace our gardens from time to time—abound in great profusion. Were it not for the lack of fragrance it would be difficult at first sight to believe that the flowers are artificial. Such,how- ever,is the case for they are the work of blind and crippled girls who have been trained to this means of earning a livelihood by the devoted workers of the Watercress and Flower Girls's Christian Mission. The story of , this noble undertaking is already well known to War-1 ringtonlans who have on two previous occasions rallied splendidly to the support of the mission. Many poo- girls whose lives would otherwise be almost burdensome i are well cared for n the homes of the organization and are enabled to become independent of charity by making the flowers for which they receive good psyment. In order to raise funds to carry on the Mission these exhibitions of the girls' handiwork are frequently held in the provinces, and as far as Warrington is con- cerned, public appreciation has hitherto been most gratifying. The present exhibition bids fair to be equally successful, for each day it has been visited by 'hundreds of townspeople, who have freely patronized the stalls. The opening ceremony on Monday afternoon was performed by the Mayoress (Mrs. Belton), the pro- ceedings being presided over by the Mayor. There were also present on the platform ;—Mr. John A. Groom (founder and superintendent of the homes), the Revs. Canon Stevenson, E. C. E. Carleton, and James Collins, and Councillors Ellison and Skelland. Mr. Gnoom, in the course of a short address, explained that the institution in whose interests they had met was founded in the year 1866, and it had for its president one of its best workers, the 7th Earl of Shaftesbury, a nobleman to whom they had not yet learnt to pay a proper debt of gratitude for his untiring efforts on behalf of the children. (Hear, hear). The watercress and flower-sellers of London were about 5,000 in num- ber. In furtherance of the aims of the mission. Many young girls who sold real flowers were inducedito for- sake that mode of earning a livelihood And to try and earn it in a more becoming and womanly way, and those who would submit to restraint were instructed in the art of domestic service, and since the beginning of the year about 3,000 had been taken from the streets, and had become domestic servants. Ths committee, in the course of the work, came across a number of girls who, in consequence of physical infirmity, v‘ ere unable to earn their living as domestic servants. As the girls were interested in the sale of natural flowers it seemed a natural transition .to make artificial ones, as the work was light, bright and cheer- ful, and it had the unspeakakable advantage that it did not press unduly upon home production. There were 100 girls in the institution, and they were submitted to a course of two year's training. From the moment they entered the institution they received a certain sum as wages; ss. 61. was the maximum ; but when he told thell audience that it was only towards the end of their term ; that they became a profit to the institution it would be sepia that they were dealing fairly if not generouslyl with the girls committed to their care. Everything al pa:earned above ss. (the charge for board and lodging) I became her own. When a girl became proficient she could earn from 10s. to 15s. a week. They took girls from all parts of the kingdom, enclitic) believed they had i one Warrington girl n the institution. Certainly there were many from Lancashire. (Applause). ' One of-the crippled girls then stepped forward and ' presented the Mayoress with a beautiful bouquet of briefly commended the work of arTtihflecimal flAyoownerhs. aving the institution, the Mayoress declared the sale open. The Rev. CANON STEVENSON, in proposing a vote of thanks to the Mayor and Mayoress, said no better, more ; delightful employment could be found for poor crippled children than that in which the girls of that institution were engaged. (Applause.) The Rev. J. CoLtiNs seconded, and the motion was ' put to the meeting and carried unanimously, his Worship briefly replying.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-05_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>CRICKET.</head>
        <p>CRICKET.</p>
        <p>WORKS COMPETITION FORMED. • ' A meeting was held at the Warrington Cricket Club Pavilion, Arpley, on Monday evening, to inaugurate a cricket competition for the various works in the town. HAYDOCK'S ONLY " RHSISTER." I Mr. C. H. Uns worth presided, and there were represents- The Rev. J. H. Riddette, Congregationalist minister. ' tives from Pearson and Knowles, itylands Bros., Cros- of Haydock, has for some years figured as the only field's, "Guardian" Office, Whitecross, Newhall passive resister of the township. The pair of spoons Engineering Co., Monks, Hall, Monks, Hall Bedstead, belonging to Mr. Riddette were formally put up for sale St. Helens Cable Co., Cheshire Lines, Richmond's, R. A. for 2s. (id., owing on the Education rate, at the Haydock Naylcrr's, Firth Co., Smethurst and Son, Wire Rope Police Office, by Mr. Gabriel Moon, on Monday. The Works, Walker's Brewery, Greening's, Corporation Staff, cost of the proceedings, 75., with the 2s. 6d. due, was at L. and N.-W. Railway, and Castle Rubber Co. once bid •, and the proceedings terminated in 14r. I Mr. H. EVANS stated that as so many clubs had Riddette thanking the auctioneer and the police for the ' entered, the competition would be on the knock-out way in which they had carried out their duties, and principle. The matches would take place,on the War. expressing the hope that this would be the last occasion rington ground on Friday evenings, on similar lines to that he would trouble them. i those which governed like competitions in East A WIDOW'S DEATH. I Lancashire. Each side would be allowed to bat 50 Mary Lswrenson, aged seventy-two, a widow, of 1, ! minutes and, in case of rain or bad tight the match Ebenezer-street, Haydock, died during Sunday night' would be finished at a date to be decided upon by the c from injuries received on the 12th inst. The deceased committee. All cricket materials would be provided by the Warrington club if necessary, and the professional lived with her son, Samuel, and on Wednesday, the 11th inst., he went to bed, his mother saying she would and some other responsible person would act as umpires. follow shortly afterwards. At 2.30 he was wakened by I A discussion arose as to the time of starting, but on a vote being taken 6.15 was decided upon. a loud noise, and found his mother at the bottom of the COMMITTEE. staircase bleeding from a wound in the head. She. gradually grew worse, and died. Mr. Jones, deputy I A committee of six was then appointed, Messrs. coroner, held an inquest at the owl's Nest on Monday' Podmore, Evans and Bibby to represent the IVarrington when a verdict of" Accidental death " was recorded, club and Messrs. Hewitt, Taylor and Duckworth to I EARLESTOWN MAN KILLED AT HA.YDOCK. reftresent the works. 1 was decided that entries close on May 7th, and the Mr. Jones, deputy county coroner, held an inquest at draw will take place the same evening W. H. Podmore, • Haydock, on Monday on James Redhead, aged forty- , Market-place, has been appointed secretary. three, a contractor, who was killed in the wood pit ! belonging to Messrs. It. Evans and2Co., early on Friday ! -----___ morning. Deceased was working with William Craw- 1. , ford, of 37, Gas-street, Newton, who said they were! I setting bars up across the roadway and at the end of i working places. At 2.15 on Friday morning, deceased was putting props under the bars, driving them in with a hammer, when the props gave wa-y,and the timber and roof came down and killed him. A verdict of" Acci- dental death " was returned.</p>
        <p>LYRIC SWEETNESS. The very word" Lyric " is suggestive of sweetness. The exquisite music of the ancient lyre was the delight of classic days, and the charming lyric songs of our age are matchless in their melody. There is a particular appropriateness, therefore, in the name which the Columbia Phonograph Company have chosen for a new attachment with which their Cylinder Graphophones are now equipped. This is the" Lyrio " reproducer. It marks a long step forward in the perfecting of the talking machine, and ensures such a beautifully sweet, I clear, and natural tone that it is really difficult to dis- DISTRICT NURSING Assoc tar roN.—The annual report , tinguish the "Lyric" reproduction from the original of the above association, which comprises Padgate, ' rendition. Several other improvements have also been Pearnhead, Woolston and other adjoining districts, has in ode ced in the 1006 models, and in order to bring just been issued. During the past year the Association these up-to-date instruments within reach of as wide a has done excellent work for it has been the means of circle as possible, the Columbia Company are prepared lightening the burden of many a home when sickness to make arrangements for the acceptance of old has been in it. The Association maintain a nurse who ' machines of any make in part exchange for their new is supported by subscriptions from the residents of the productions. Full particulars, together with "Price above districts.—Mr.and Mrs.Buckton have particularly Book 20 " which tell all about the "Lyric " Grapho- taken a great deal of interest in the work of the phones and some new disc models, may be had free from &amp;ssociation and have been most generous supporters to the Columbia Phonograph Company, General, 80, Great holscheme. Eastern-street, London, E.C.</p>
        <p>r</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-05_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON</p>
        <p>Index to Inside Pages.</p>
        <p>PAGE.</p>
        <p>2. Warrington Dock Question. Women's Chat. "A. Son of Empire " (Serial). 3. Our Local Churches. Town Council. Football. 6. Peace Meeting. Beautiful Warrington. Crippled Girls Floral Exhibition. Rural Council. Children's Cornr. 7. Cycling Gossip. Great Sankey Affairs. . .</p>
        <p>Cricket. PUBLIC BENEFIT, UNDER TFIE CLOCK FOR BERT BOOTS Di LANCASIIIRE.—Advt. Notice. - - -</p>
        <p>_ _ The Warrington "Examiner" is now published on Friday afternoon, price one penny, and is on sale at the various newsagents at about 4 o'clock. Should any difficulty be experienced in getting the paper the proprietors will undertake to &amp;liver a copy at any address in Warrington by 5 o'clock on Friday after- noon without extra charge. Address all communica, tions to" Examiner Office," Bewsey-street Warrington PUBLIC BENEFIT.—UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS I'S LANCASHIRE.—Advt. Personal. At a meeting of the House of Laymen of the Con- vocation of York on Thursday, Colonel J. D. Buckton, Warrington, was appointed a member of the Standing Committee. Message from Mr. H. Whltham.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday we were gratified to receive a post- card from Mr. H. Whitham, which sets at rest all fears for the safety of himself and family at San Francisco. The post•card was posted at San Francisco on April 20th and reads as follows : "Please notice, family and self all safe. Home seems to be pretty. well wrecked. City nearly all destroyed, fire and quake." Boy Breaks His Thigh. Herbert Hayes, the five-year-old'son of Mr'. Herbert Hayes, manager of the Junior Conservative Club, was going through the 37;•-ard at home on Saturday morning when a gust of wind blew a large board on top of him. The boy was knocked down and, unfortunately, broke his right thigh. He ,was removed to the Infirmary for treatment. New Rural Dean. _</p>
        <p>The Bishop of Liverpool (Dr. Chevasse) has appainted the Rev. H. Gibson Smith, vicar of Alter- ton, as the new Rural Dean of Childwall, in succes- sion to Archdeacon Spoouer. Mr. Smith was at one time second master of the Warrington Grammar School, and was well known and highly respected in Warrington. LAWN Mowsits, Garden Rollers, Garden Tools, Lawn Mower repairs a speciality.—J. S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge st.,Warrington.—Advt. Peter Pan. There is still another week of " Peter Pan " at the Shakespeare Theatre, Liverpool. Hundreds of War- rington theatre-goers have probably not been to sea the play either from apathy, or a doubt whether it will suit their taste. We have been, and we can tell them that their apathy is a crime, and their taste woefully uncultivated if they cannot enjoy fully as much as the most imaginative youngster a trip to fairy land under the guidance of whimsical fascinating "Peter Pan."/ Mr. Barrie's play has a charm for grown-ups and children alike which is as much beyond argument as it is exquisite. Nothing more charming, pretty and fairy-like has, we imagine, ever been staged. The acting of Miss Cecilia Loftus and Miss [Hilda Trevelyan is extraordinarily good, while the real ' pirates and red-skins are convincingly wicked and fierce. No pantomime can compare with " Peter Pan." We put it in a new class of stage play all by itself. I UNDER THE CLOCK for Good Boors.—Adift. Coming Events. Under the auspices of the association of the Vhurch of England schools, a meeting of church people will be held in the Parr Hall on Friday next. The Bishop of Liverpool will preside and the principal speaker will be Lord Hugh Cecil. A licensing meeting will be held at the Police Court on the 24th inst,when the Compensation Authority will be prepared to hear persons interested in the licenses which it is proposed shall be extinguished. The licences, are the Rope and Anchor Inn.Buttermarket- street, Queen's Hotel, Sankey-street, and the Baar's Head Inn, Pig Hill. The annual sale of work in aid of St. Barnabas Church extension fund will be held in the Assembly Room at the Conservative Club on Wedarssday and Thursday next. It will be opened on Wednesday afternoon by Nirs. John White. Various attractions will be provided.</p>
        <p>LADLES' BEST GLACE BOOTS AND SHOES, UNDER TICS CLOCK, BRIDGE-STREET.—Advt. The After Care of Female Inebriates. As the operations of the Police Court and Prison Gate Mission of the Diocesan c.E.T.s„ are conducted at every police and petty sessional court in Liverpool, and South-west Lancashire, its opportunities for use- fulness are unique. Arrangements have now been completed with the committee of the Lancashire Inebriate Reformatory at Whalley, near BlAckburn, for undertaking the after care of inebriates discharged from that institution. The gratuity will be paid through the Mission, and those women who have no ' friends will he received at the C.E.T.S. Women's Temperance Home in Liverpool. A thorough super- ' vision will be exercised over all men and women sent ' to the Mission. Persons discharged on recognizances under the First Offenders Act are regularly being released into the care of the missionaries, and are visited and reported upon to the bench. During the month of April, 230 visits were paid to the police courts ; 1,275 persons spoken to in the courts and cells and 010 persons met there were subsequently visited in their abodes, 43 women, girls and boys were taken to homes, restored to friends or provided with work ; 81 persons from the courts were assisted at the request of the magistrates and 43 at the sugges- tion of the court officials ,• 20 men were offered work at the Society's Firewood and Mat Factory, Bootle, from the courts and prisons ; 204 persons were inter- viewed at the Society's Offices and in the homes of the various missionaries, 178 letters were written with reference to persons being assisted. At the Mission room, near Walton Prison 597 persons were provided with breakfast and helped in other ways on discharge from the jail, and 7'2 visits were paid ; 117 other persons were visited in their homes at the request of friends to warn and otherwise prevent the necessity for police proceeding--215 total abstinence pledges were administered, 40 meetings were addressed, &amp;c., &amp;C. LEA-FRANCIS CYCLES—Britain's Best. Prices reduced, Demand increased. Inspection invited. Sole agent :—J. S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge st., War- rington.-4dvt.</p>
