      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-05_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>WARRINGTON FOOTBALL CLUB. REVIEW OF THE SEASON 1905-6</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON FOOTBALL CLUB. REVIEW OF THE SEASON 1905-6</p>
        <p>By " Ovalist."</p>
        <p>The Rugby football season which has just closed can be summed up in one brief alliterative term. It has been one of manifest mediocrity —not only so far as Warrington is concerned but in connection with almost the whole of the Northern Union programme. The new system of calculating the League champion- ship by a percentage of results on matches played has never become popular, mainly because the public does not like its football fare served up in the shape of mathematical problems. Then the formation of the League itself was inherently bad. Thirty-one clubs were lumped together and left to arrange fixtures as they liked so long as ten fixtures (home and away) were arranged with clubs which were previously in the old First Division. The consequence has been that some clubs have played 20 matches and othere3B or 40, while none have had the same fixtures. The positions indicated by the percentages have, there- fore, been no real guide to form at all, and the com- petition has concluded with a crowning anomaly. Leigh are hailed as champions by virtue of a percent- age of 80, obtained from 30 fixtures. They hold a false position, inasmuch as they have not met such clubs as Hunslet (runnets-up), Bradford (cup-holders), Leeds, Halifax and Hull, their only journeys into Yorkshire being to Hudderefield and Hull (Kingston Rovers). It is safe to say that had Leigh undertaken fixtures with these clubs, as did Warrington and most of the Lancashire Clubs, they would have finished up v'ery much lower in the table than they are to-day. Naturally the public did not take a great deal of interest in the matches played under a championship competition which paved the way to such a farcical finale, and nearly every club in the League, including Warrington. has to lament a decrease of revenue and the loss of considerable public favonr. It has long been an open secret that the Warrington men had no intention of trying for the League championship this season. They argued—and it is only human if not the bsst sportsmanship —that it was no good them exerting themselves to such a purpose seeing that Leigh and other clubs had arranged a much easier list of fixtures, in all proba- bility Warrington could not have succeeded no matter how they had tried, for clubs like Oldham, Hunslet, and Leeds have all made great effoete and failed for the same reason. I hope we have seen the last of such a bastard League system as has been in opera- tion during the season just closed. If there was one thing which the Warrington men (I.d take an interest in it was the Northern Union Cup, and there were hopes that the trophy might be retained for another year. In all probability it would have been, but for an unfortunate slip at Batley in the very first round when Warrington lost by a point after having the beet of the game. Had they won they would have been exceptionally well-favoured by the succeeding draws, and progress to the semi-final at least would have been uninterrupted. The causes of that harrowing defeat are too freshly remembered to need recapitulating here. There was some had management and a lot of bad luck. The latter has dogged the Warrington men's footsteps all through the season. Since the first match in the season the Club has never been able to place a fall team in the field on two successive Saturdays. All the cracks have been " off " injured f.r long periods, and the back division has in consequence been totally diserganised. But for the excellence of the reserves, and the strength of the forwards, the Club must have sunk very low in competitive football. Fish, from whom much was hoped after the training he had gone thrtaitgh for sprinting, sustained a broken collar-bone at Wigan in a County match on October 7th. He had only just got fit again by the New Year when he again fell a victim to iniury on the same ground in the N.U. " international ' on January Ist., and a damaged leg kept him out of the team until March 10th. The "irrepressible " has thus played in very few matches, and the loss to the Club has teen irreparable. Isher- wood, Dickinson, Hocicenhall, Harris, and Kenyon have all suffered at different times, while the two last-named have also lost much of the form which earned them distinction /last year. Every- thing considered, Warrington's percentage of 53.01 from 38 matches is not at all discreditable. The most gratifying features of the campaign are the con- tinued good form of the forwards generally, and the improvement shown in this department by young players like Harmer and Swift. Among the reserve backs a real find has been unearthed in Berry, who on being promoted from the centre position in the "A" team to full hack for the first team showed remarkable capabilities, and is to-day considered the most promis- ing custodian in Lancashire. He should worthily fill the shoes of steady -Tack Hallam for many seasons to ceme. Jordan and Brooks have both been useful reserves, and the importation of Taylor, Lees and Lambert has been a capital investment. The last- named, who is quite a youth, strikes me as being the best young half-back Warrington have obtained for some years, °end I have high hopes of his future. Widdeson, NYE° was secured in the middle of the season, Billowed none of his old Brongh- ton form and was a great disappointment to officials and spectators alike. Perhaps the greatest need of the club is another threequarter or two with pace. Well as some of the old hands have been playing. they are not running as spkdily as of yore, and on the right wing in particular a fast threequarter is an urgent necessity. In other departments there is reserve talent of a very high quality, as has been proved be the way the "A" team has again carried off the Combination Champion- ship,and from a playing sense at any rate the future of the Chth is bright with possibilities. ' What we require most of all is legislation by the Union which shall make competitions real competitions, and the football of a quality such as will once more win the support of the crowd. An analysis of the season's matches shows that, counting the game at Liverpool, and the cnp-ties with Rochdale Hornets and Leigh (Lancashire Cup) and Batley (Northern Union Cup), Warrington have played 42 matches, of which they hays won 20, lost 18, and drawn 4. They have scored 52 goals, 67 tries, 305 Points, against 36 goals, 46 tries, 210 points. Their 'biggest victory was over Widnes, whom on March 14th they beat by 3I points to 2, while their heaviest defeat was, strange to say, on the Warrington ground on Sep- tember 16th, when they went down before Broughton I Rangers by 10 points to nil, chiefly owing to the grand • form of Wilson on that day. They have lost live' l matches on the Wilderspool ground this season, the 'clubs which were successful being Leigh (Lancashire cup-tie), Bronehton Rangers, Wigan, Hull Kingston Rovers, and Salford. Last season they lost once at I home—to Oldham. They have won six matches away i from home, a slight falling awag from last season. The clubs who were thus beaten by Warrington were Rochdale (twice), Widnes, St. Helens, Salford and Millom. To sum np. Warrington have been twice !beaten by Leigh, Broughton Rangers, Wigan and Hull ! Kingston Rovers. They have been once beaten by ' Oldham, Bradford. Runcorn, Swinton, Dewsbury, Salford, Leeds, Hull and Batley (cup-tie). Once each I have Warrington beaten Leigh, Oldham, Barrow, Brad- ford, Runcorn, Swinton, Dewsbury, Salford, Leeds, Hunslet and Hull. They have beaten the following twice :—Widnes, St. Helens, and Millom, while Roch- dale Hornets have been beaten three times—once in a cup-tie. They have drawn twice with Halifax, and once each with Barrow and Runcorn. -</p>
        <p>---..- ....... The results of each match played are as follows :- 1905. For Agst. Sept. 2 Lelgh • home 7 0 „ 9 Oldham home 2 0 , 15 Broughton „ 23 Wigan away 3 11 „ 30 Halifax away 10 10 Oct. 7 Barrow home 18 0 ~ 9 Hull Kingston T. home 5 8 9. 14 Rochdale (cup-tie) away 10 0 „ 16 Bradford , „ 21 Oldham away 0 8 , 23 Leigh (cup-tie) home 2 5 Nov. 4 Runcorn.... away 2 5 „ 11 Rochdale „ 25 Swinton Dec. 2 Widnes n 9 Runcorn „ 15 Dewsbury away 5 G „ 23 St. Helens home 18 0 , 25 Leigh '„ 26 Salford home 4 5 '„ 27 Hunslet . .. 30 Broughton ............... away 0 7 1906 Jan. 1 Dewsbury „ 6 Leeds „ 13 Hull „ 20 Swinton 27 Salford away 8 6 Feb. 3 Millom home 29 5 „ 10 Millom away 10 0 „ 17 1-lull Kingston R ~ 24 Barrow ', 27 Bradford Mar. 3 Batley •••• away 5 G „ 10 Wigan " home 3 6 • 17 Runcorn Liverpool 15 15 ~ 19 Leeds away 0 3 „ 24 Widnes „ 31 Rochdale Hornets away 3 0</p>
        <p>Apl. 7 Hull „ 13 Halifax (G.F) home 2 2 „ 16 St. Helens away 8 0 „ 28 Huns let The completed Northern League tVile is as follows : For. Ag,st. Per- P. W. L. 13. P. G. T. P. G. T. P. centage- Leigh Hunslet 32 25 7 05068 78 370 20 33 143 78•12 Leeds Oldham 4O 28 10 253 61108 446 28 23 125 7210 Keighley 2B 19 8 13939 59 255 25 38 164 6964 Wigan Hull E.R 36 22 11 34739 56 243 46 42 218 6527 Bro'ton R 3421 12 143 50100 400 39 43 222 6313 Halifax— 38 20 10 84845 57 261 41 50 232 6315 Runcorn audleld 3O 17 11 23633 54 224 27 40 174 6000 Swinton 32 17 12 33728 49 203 27 38 168 5781 Bradford 34 19 13 24054 89 371 35 43 199 5715 St. Helens 30 16 13 13335 58 244 34 48 212 5500 Warrington 38 19 16 34145 61 273 29 42 184 53.94 Wak'fleld T 3213 15 43037 88 188 38 62 262 4687 Hull 36 16 19 13347 70 304 33 48 220 4513 Salford.-- 34 14 17 33137 66 272 48 58 270 4518 Pontefract 28 11 16 12335 47 211 32 44 196 41.07 Batley 34 11 18 52740 31 173 40 45 215 39.70 Widnes 08 10 16 22213 33 129 31 GO 212 3928 Dewsbury 36 13 21 22827 33 162 42 55 252 3888 Bromley York 34 11 21 2.24 37 32 170 39 57 219 3529 Barrow 32 919422 18 34 138 45 78 321 3117 Normanton 24 413 210 811 49 41 66 230 2013 Cistleford.. 20 3 15 2 8 12 7 45 47 77 325 1620•00 Nlillom 2O 315291615 77 47 73 328 2000 Rochdale H 32 323 61212 27 105 39 83 327 18.75 Nforecambe 26 220 '4 812 25 99 36 70 282 1518 Brighouseß 25 321 2 815 19 87 46 81 335 1518 The points /ire two for a win and ooe for a draw. The percentage is calculated upon the basis of the total points possible. •</p>
        <p>The South-West Lancashire League Cup has bean won by Wigan for the seoorrl year in succession. Last year Warrington were runners-up—only a point behind Wigan—but this year they have to be content with third place. The positions of the clubs are :</p>
        <p>Points. P. W. L. D. For Agst. Pts. Wigan Leigh Warrington lO ... 6 ... 4 ... 0 ... 81— 31 ... 12 Ftnne,orn , 10... 5... 8... 1 ... 33 72 ... 7 Widnes lO .. 3 ... 7 ... 0 ... 34 93 ... 6 St. Helens The following is Warrington's record in the Northern Union League and Lancashire Senior Competition since the foundation of the Union.</p>
        <p>For. Against. P. W. L. D. P. P. Total. 1895-6 12 ... 17 ... 20 ... 5 ...198 ...240 ... 39 P. W. L. D. G. T. P. G. T. P. P. 1896-7 28...11...10... 5-12...16...100-17...17...124...27 1897—S 26...10...14... 2...13 _34...128_28_ 42...178_22 1893-9 26...11 .14... 1...183413131'q217 23 1899-00 26...12...13... 1...24 ..42-174-16...32...128...25 1900—i 26-12...11.... 3...25...33...149...18...3.0...126-27 *l9Ol-2 25...14...12... 0...24...38-162...27...32...1.50-26 1902-3 31.:.14...13... 7...23...34...149...28 —36_161...35 1903—i 34...17...14... $...29...52-214...27...33...153...37 1904-5 31_20-12_ 2...34...48...220 —30_30...150_42 1905-6 3S-19...16... 3...45...61...273...29 ..42...184-41 [Percentage 53•91pts. *Two points derlacted for breach of professional rules.</p>
        <p>The following is a list of the first team individual scorers:—</p>
        <p>Goals. Tries. Points. Preston., 2G ... 7 ... 73 Fish Hockenhall Harmer Jordan Kenyon Thomas 1 ... 4 ... 14 Harris Heath Taylor Swift 1 ... 3 ... 11 Isherwoood. Brookes Knight Widdeson Lees Dickenson Jones C. Ashton Cook Boardman Davies</p>
        <p>Preston heads the list this year with 26 goals 7 tries 73 points. Fish being a good second to the burly for- ward. Haying been off the team a6ovo half the season he has a very creditable score. He has also scored 2 tries for the County. The backs have only just beaten the forwards in scoring, i.e.</p>
        <p>Backs Forwards ,</p>
        <p>Goals. Tries. Points. 26 ... 39 ... 169 29 ... 26 ... 136</p>
        <p>The "A "team has again had a remarkably successful season, whichterminated on F,ster Monday with a victory over St. Helena "A " which secured the Com- bination championship for Warrington the second year in succession. Moreover the scoring average for the team isa record in the history of the club. Thirty matches have been played during the season, 26 being won,three lost (Chadderton,friendly,Oldham and Wigan) and one drawn. The aggregate score is 76 goals, 131 tries, 515 points, against 16 goals, 23 tries, 101 points, In three successive matches no less than 168 points to 8 points were scored by the Champions, which is doubt- less another record.</p>
        <p>The complete table of the Lancashire Rugby Com- bination is as follows:</p>
        <p>W. L. D. Pts. Warrington "A" Wigan "A" lO ... 3 ... 1 ... 21 Chadderton 9 ... 3 ... 2 ... 20 Oldham "A" Broughton Ranger,s "A" Rochdale Hornet's "A" Pemberton, St.l3elen's "A" 2 .11 ... 1 ... 5 The following is a list of the " A" team scorers. Goals. Tries, Points. Kenyon ll ... 14 ... Knight Jordan Garside Foster Jones Lloyd Davies Cartwrighb Brookes Lambert Short Hewitt Hallam Harris Marshmart Bibby Turner Harmer Tsherwood W. Ashton Hartley Shaw Fairhurst Bennett Swift Lees Dickenson Berry</p>
        <p>Assessment Committee. A meeting of the Warrington 13,nion 'Assessment Committee was held at the Workh-me on Wednesday. Mr. A. J. Jolley was re-elected chairman and Mr. J. Billinge vice-chairman for the ensuing year. Some discussion arose as to the time of meeting, Mr. Bent suggesting that in future the meetings of the com- mittee should take place at the conclusion of interim meetings of the Board. This proposal was considered impracticable and it was decided to adhere to the present system of meeting on the first Friday in each month.</p>
