        <head>DON'T NEGLECT AN INJURY! ' Festering and D 158333 Lark in Tiny Cuts—Keep Zam-Bak Uandy</head>
        <p>DON'T NEGLECT AN INJURY! ' Festering and D 158333 Lark in Tiny Cuts—Keep Zam-Bak Uandy</p>
        <p>Accidents min happen, and Itte necessity of keeping a t reliable first-aid such as Zam-BA handy, at home and at work, cannot be too strongly insisted upon. Simple injuries are liable to fester and breed disease germs if not properly cared for. Many cases of chronic eczema or ulceration are traceable to neglected cuts or ' small abrasions, and blood poisonitrr', which endangers both life end limb, very frequently develops from the most trivial scratch. Neither housewife, mother, nor bread-winner can afford to rialc these cornolicationa ; and besides, it is often both desirable and essential that a wound shonld ! be robbed of its smarting pain and healed up quickly. Zarn-Buk is not only a soothing and rapid healer, but a ! powerful antiseptic which purifies, prevents poisoning, kills microbes, and resists all tendency to putrefaction. Zam-Bak is a pure healing balm made from rich medicinal herbs, the saps and juices of which are cleverly combined and concentrated, and wonderfully refined. Many modern preparations contain quantities I of animal fat and mineral poisons, and are not only I coarse, irritating, and unreliable, but tend to create the ' very complications which should be guarded against. The Zam-Buk way is both different and superior. 'lhis , natural healing balm takes out the smart and soothes I the pain, reduces inflammation, and induces al clean and I rapid healing right away. If poisening or disease haa already developed, ZATII- I Bilk is even mere valnable. Careful applications over the I affected area of tissue prove cooling, soothing and cleansing. Irritation ceases, burning sores cease to torture. interne matter and all elements of disease are I expelled, and finelly Zara-Balt helos Nature to build up new tisane to replace that 'which has bran damaged, 1 diseased or destroyed. New cells appear almost visibly and finally 7, trn-Bnk covers the once raw or broken surface with new, clear, healthy skin, often leaving not the faintest scar. Mrs. C. NI man, of 47. North now, Ttoose, Barrow-in- Furness, says :—" I had the misfortune to scald my hand while cooking. For weeks the place obstinately refused to heal, and kept on festering. Hearing about Zarn-Buk I resolved to try it. I was most pleasantly surprised, for throngh using Zion-Bak my hand COM- meneed to heal rapidly, and was better within a week. I consider Zarn-link to be the finest healing balm I have ever used for scalds or bin ns." Also invaluable for cots, bruises, abraaions,gatherings, poisoned wounds,skin diseases, sprains, piles, &amp;c. Every home needs a box.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-05_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>WARRINGTON RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>Mr. Brookes and the Dust Nuisance.</p>
        <p>The annual meeang of the Warrington Rural District Council was held at Bewsev Chambers on Wednesday when there were proseat : Dr. Murray, Messrs, Brooks J. Mather, J. Thompson, J. W. Knowles, J. Backhouse, W. ifonghton, .J. BAB, J. C. Sutton (clerk), B. S. Newcombe (surveyor). H. H. Damage (assistant surveyor), F. W. kerfoot (inspector) and Dr. Sephton (medical officer). ELECTION OF OFFICERS. MR. BROOKS AND TUE DUST NUISANCE.</p>
        <p>--- Pending the election of chairtnan the clerk took the . chair and congratulated the Council upon being able to commence the half year with the lowest call in the history of the Board. Mr. liNcoartss moved the re-election of Mr. Brookes as chairman, Mr. Mather seconded and the motion was unanimously approved. Mr. BROOKS returned thanks for his appointment. They were starting the new year under fairly favour- able circumst owes. The death rate was very low and would compare well with such health resorts as Black- pool, Southport, and Morecambe and other places. However they would not advertise their district as a health resort as Blackpool district. The death rate for the past year was the lowest that had existed during the time the Council bad bran a highway authority. Their roads were at present in a state of transition. They did not know exactly where they were. Up to the present they had had a good name for roads, and he hoped they would retain it, but new methods of trifle —the heavy motor engines and their loads—were play- ing havoc with the made. They did not know what to expect in the way of loads—whether there would be any limit on the highways or whether the Council must prepare for all that came. They would hardly be able to calculate what their roads were going to cost them, but there was one feature which was attracting universal attention. That was the dust problem. He did not know whether any of them had taken sarunles of grass and crops some distance away from the roads, but he had had the opinion of several experienced farmers who told him that the cereal crops, the pasturage and the hay crops by the roadside were practically ruined; in fact for every mile of higtiway through rural districts there was a sufficient quantity of land to makea decent sized farm practically one of cultivation. That was a very serious state of things end one which they must use their best endeavours to solve in conjunction with other rural districts. They would have to take action as the County Council he was afraid did not come into touch to the same degree with the forming interests as they did, and he prould certainly anggeist that in the near future they 'should ask the Rural District Cobncils' Association to consider the question as they were as likely a body as they could approach to arrive at a solution of that very difficult _problem. fie hoped personally that some inexpensive method might be dis- covered, for preventing the nuisance. If they had to incur the expense which many districts bad had to meet he did not think the ratepayers should be called upon to bear the whole of the expense as the nusiance was not caused by local residents but by pleasure seekers from all over the kingdom, and if anything of a very expensive character was necessary to moderate the nuisan?e they should have some very substantial con- tribution from the National Exchequer. He hoped that the Rural District Council's Association would he approached by a strong resolution from that Council in the near future. Mr. KNow•CRI said he would like to say something had he not been out of order. The Chairman's opinion ' was not the opinion of the whole Council and he would not like his remarks to be put into print (several mem- bers" we hops it is.") Mr. BROOKS : lem asking that the Council should dismiss the matter at a future meeting. Dr. MurtftAv was re-elected vice-chairman. In answer to Iqr. \father, Mr. NVWCONIRE said that solutions and eranlsions for treating the roads in order to prevent dust were luxuries pure and simple. The only satisfactory solution was to reconstract whole roads with tarred macadam. CONFRRENCR DELEGATES. The Chairman and the Clerk were appointed to repre- sent the Council at the conference of the Rural District Council Acsociation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-05_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>TEIE WORK OF A FREE CR7RCEI COUNT:7AL.</head>
        <p>TEIE WORK OF A FREE CR7RCEI COUNT:7AL.</p>
        <p>London: Thomas Law. 11161horial Hall,Farri ngdon-street, National Council of Evangelical Free Churches. The span volume of 170 pagea is mainly the work of the Assistant Secretary of the National Free Church Connell. It is divided into six parts, and deals with (1) The Council and its Machinery, 2) The regular work of a Council, (3) The special work of a Council, (4) Things that help, (5) The work of a Federation, and (6) General. Apart from it being a valuable manual for secretaries, it is at once an evidence of the underlying unity of the Free Churches, the magnitude of their machinery, and the amplitude of their work. A good inclek lenders the book additionally useful.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-05_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>HUGE CARGO OF GOLD.</head>
        <p>HUGE CARGO OF GOLD.</p>
        <p>The Weimer Castlekwhich arrived at Southampton on Saturday,bronght home one of the tirgest consignments of gold ever shipped from South Africa. It was valued at £950,600, and was transferred to a train in waiting, which proceeded to London.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-05_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>CONSTIPATION CURED.</head>
        <p>CONSTIPATION CURED.</p>
        <p>Bile Beans End Life-long Suffering.</p>
        <p>Doctors agree that constipation is the cause of quite two-tnirds of the serious ailments which form their daily prtctice, espetially among women. Habitual con- stipation lays the body open to all sorts of deadly dis- ease. Bile Beans cure constipation in the only sure way by regulating and strengthening all the bodily functions and promoting the flow of all Vie digestive juices. blrs. Mice Sully, of 83, Lion-road, Bedminster, Bristol, says :—" I hay!' been more or less an invalid all my life, being subibct to constipation, but more especially during ths last three years. I was always weak and languid, and at last I got to such a state that I did not want to see/anyone or do anything. I was always in the doctor's hands. I also suffered considerably from • indigestion. I couldeat practically nothing, and would simply pick at my food. "I paid pounds to the doctors, but got no bettor, and as I was so ill and there was consumption in the family I nsed to greive abont it. After I bad tried different kinds of medicine without any good results, I decided to see what Bile Beans would do. I derived great benefit from the beans, and when I had finished a coume I found that the constipation was banished. Bile Beans have done me a world of good, and I feel sure nothing • else could have brought about such a perfect cure." Bile Beans, the world's most successful tonic-laxative, cure all forms of liver, stomach and bowel disorder., Sold only in sealed boxes, of all chemists, or—post free from the Bile Bean Manufacturing Co., 1, Red Cross- street,London, E.C., at Is. IA., and 2s. 9d. (triple size).</p>
