      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-05_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>PEACE MEETING AT WARRINGroN.</head>
        <p>PEACE MEETING AT WARRINGroN.</p>
        <p>"MILITARISM AND PRESENT DAY IDEALS."</p>
        <p>Replies to the National Servize League.</p>
        <p>" Militarism and present day ideals" was the subject under consideration at a peace meeting held in the Parr Hall on Friday evening. There was only a small attendance, Mr. C. Jackson Hoboes presiding. The principal speakers were Mr. A. J. King, MP. for the Knutsford division, and MIAs Fruves Thompson (president Of the Liverpool and Birkenhead Women's Peace and Arbitration Society). Amongst those present were the Revs. John 'lunge and C. Harvey Cook, Mrs. Holmes, Mr. J. Longland, and qr. W. Roberts. There was." only a small attendance of the general public.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-05_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>REV. E. C. E. CARLETON'S VIEWS.</head>
        <p>REV. E. C. E. CARLETON'S VIEWS.</p>
        <p>The following letter had been received from the Rev.' E. C. E. Carl-ton :—" Dear sir.—l am obliged by your note of the 20th inst. inviting me to the peace meeting in the Parr Hall, which fear I cannot attend. I am not so sure that it is wise for people to be encouraged to direct their minds to the subject of international peace, while the peace that is more within our power, domestic peace, sdbial peace, Ste., seems rather neglected. The Gospel plan is to begin with the individual, and work from within, outwards. When each individual in the nation has been taught the art of living at peace with his neighbours, then the nation itself will be in a positiou to make its best, because roost consistent, contribution to the peace of the world, and et-operate with other nations to that end. If this plan is not adopted, and we satisfy our- selves wlth discussing far away scheme.; of universal, peace, we may be affording an opportunity to insincere people of making professions which they have no idea of putting into practice. " The eyes of a fool are in the ends of the eleth." Personally, lam not so sure that the declared policy of many of those promoting your meeting is not calculated to bring about a disturb- ance of peace both at home and abroad; and for that I reason I should have adi lculty in co-operating with 1 them consistently in this matter. I may say that the 1 prayer for peace is never omitted in the service of the Church of England.—Yours truly, E. C. E. Canr.wrox." THE CHAIRMAN AND THE NATIONAL SERVICE I -LEAGUE. I</p>
        <p>The CFIAIRMAN said it seemed to have been thought by many people in Warrington that this was to be a political meeting. He, therefore, wished to say at once that that was not the case as was generally understood by the word "political." The subject was of far greater moment than anything which might be settled by a political organization. They, the Society of Friends, small body as they were, especially in this 1 town, had always taken a forward position in all matters relating to the promotion of peace principles,and as they had heard so much in Warrington about the National Service League, it was thought well that their fellow-townsmen should have the opportunity of con- sidering the subject from an entirely different aspc3t. Warrington seemed to be the centre or headquarters of this National Service League, or compulsory military training, at any rate so far as the chief exponent of the scheme was concerned, but so far as his (the Chairraan's) reading of the debates had gone, he thoaght when there had been a resolution put to the meeting, a vote in favour of that proposed system had generally been lost. They deplored the fostering of the militaryspirit as was evidenced by the formation of Boys' Brigades in 1 all directions, and by the desire for military drill in many qf their educational establish- ments. These some What new methods did not tend to create a peaceful spirit at rind. but on the contrary, were likely to arouse a a rlike feeling, and to him the strangest part of the busine.• was that this encourage- merit to war should be supported by some ministers of religion. (Hear, hear.) Believing e- he did that all war was un-Christian, and therefore unlawful, it com- pletely passed his understanding that men of high attainments intellectua"y, ettga-,ed daily in the perusal of the Scriptures, fully conversant with the New Testa- ment in which they were supposed to believe, could be found to attend military functions, the launching of huge war vessels, built specially for the destruction of human life in the most wholesale manner possible, I rendering homeless many thousands of women and children. They had been told often that the way to keep the peace was to be prer zed ror war. That was a fallacy easily exposed. If t; )5, 1.c..t on building new men-of-war, increasing the S ading Army at home and abroad, it was perfectly certain other nations would do the same. They could not carry out these increases without adding considerably to officers of all grades, and during a war and after a war promotion was rapid. Therefore it was natural that a most dangerous fighting spirit would prevail and quarrels be provoked. (Applause). THE GROWTH OF ARBITRATION.</p>
        <p>Miss THOMPSON then delivered an interesting address. She said all honoured the bravery, &amp;urage, self-denial and heroism of our ioldiers, but when it was asserted that because s .r brought out those great qualities we should, to a cerZain oxtent, suppori. it, they were ready to say "What about our doctors, our clergy, our nurses, our policemen, and firemen, who showed, in private life, heroism of the most exalted kind in what was called cold blood." (Idear, hear.) Since the Boer war a nave had been sprea ling over the land in favour et arbitra- tion for the settlement of interrational disputes. During the last three years forty-three arbitration treaties had been arranged between nations, and since arbitration came into vogue in 1798 there had been no fewer than 540 cases of stkccessful arbitration. At present, though there were certainly storm clouds on the horizon in some directions. we I were at peace, and yet this time of all others was ' chosen by Lord Roberts to bring forward his szheme for a million men in the services and the training of the boys in our schools in military exercLes. Miss Thompson contrasted the objects of Boys' Brigades with those of the 170 Life Brigades which bad been formed. While the ideal of the first was to Vo ready if necessary to take life that of the Life Brigade was on every possible occasion to save life. Soldiering was only one of ninny professions, and why give special time to that in schools any more than to teach elementary law, or medicine, or any other , technical knowledge? She thought the higher ideal was to telch to save life, and not to he ready to take life, and military training given to children would tend to implant the seed of jealousy, and possibly envy and hatred of other nations, instead of a cosmopolitan generosity to the world at large in accordance with the idea of universal brotherhood. (Applause.) Whit she wanted to see implanted in the children were thoughts' of the goad that there was in all the nations and the feeling that by combination and federation the various' qualities bestowed upon the nations could be so used I that everything we wanted to see in the world could be I brought about. If we were to go back to the ape and tiger stage and encourage fighting instincts we would bmoving back the fingers on the dial plate of time. It seemed to her that anyone who believed in a real religion must admit that it must go through the whole of their lives. She was utterly unable to understand' how anyone could declare that it was right on one occasion to speak the truth and be honourable and on another it was to3bo diametrically opposite. If the\ killing of ono man was murder, the killing of a thou- sand men was a thousand murders, and moreover they were killing innocent men. She felt that present day ideals were moving up to a higher plane hut on the 1 one question of militarism we were ignoring the very points we were endeavouring to carry out in other directions. If we followed the teaching of Christ it was utterly impossible to condone war. If only the Churches of England came over to the peace side there would be an end of war. (Applause.) Mr. KING, M.P., who was received with applause, said there was no questiori in which he was so deeply interested as that of the substitution of arbitration for war in the settlement of international questions. They had entered upon the twentieth century, and professed to he a Christian people, believing in a religion founded by " The Prince of Peace," who taught as his central doctrine the brotherhood of men. As to the reconcil- ability of war with the profession of Christianity he ' would say in the words of John Bright : "That is a matter which every man must settle for himself. If he wishes to know of a book which says 14, great deal on the subject I can recommend the Netv Testament." War was simply a trial of strength, and much depended on which nation had the longest purse, and often before a war was over the belligerents became so exhausted that they agreed to a conference, and settled the points in dispute, whereas if they had met before the outbreak the Sallle results would have been obtained without cost and the loss of life. There were I now, however, many causes of encourage- ment in the direction of arbitration. (Applause.) The mass of the people were better educated, and they were beginning to recognize more and more the solidarity of the mass of the people of different nations and to see that whoever else benefitted by war they did not. We had also in this country a very great force on the side of peace in the person of the King. There was, unfortunately, far too prevalent the fallacy that in order to ensure peace we must prepare for war. The result was that the great powers of Europe were every year piling on the expense and the taxation necessary to keep up their armaments to such an extent that the burden on the people was becoming more and more intolerable. In 1894 the great nations of Europe and America (leaving out Japan) spent £18,030,000 on naval expenditure, and in 1899 it had grown to £08,000,000, and that increase led the Powers to consider what could be done to reduce it. The result was the Hague Con- ference, which had settled four disputes, and yet five years after it was dissolved the naval expenditure had increased to £101,000,000. When was all that expenditure to stop and how much better were we for it? (Hear, hear, and applause). The navel and military expendi- ture of this country had doubled during the last ten years, and we could not keep on doing that kind of thing indefinitely. During the present session of Par- liament many reselutions had been carried in favour of urgently needed 'measures of social reform. The great difficulty they were faced with in dealing with</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-05_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>Examiner. SP turday, My 5, 1906.</head>
        <p>The Examiner. SP turday, My 5, 1906.</p>
        <p>them was where to get the money from. So long as we were spending 143 millions a year-100 millions of which was spent either in preparation for war or in payinit interest and sinking fund on the old debt which was the result of previous wars—it did not leave very much to be spent on social reform. There had been a great spread in recent years of the spirit of militarism, even in Our schools. The greatness of this country depended not upon the skill which its manhood attained in destroying their tellow-men, but upon a sympathy with other nations, and statesmen, when discussing difficulties with foreign Power 3, should put themselves in the position of those powers. Alluding to the Nation 4 Service League, Mr. King said he did not think the country would ever stand conscription or compulsory , military service all round. There might, however, be [certain advantages in the system from the peace point of view, as it would put their principles to a severe 1 test and they would either have to go to prison or conform to the law. Personally, if he were as passive resister he would much rather be one on the war question than on education, but it would he very difficult to distinguish what part of one's rate went to war expenditure. Mr. King answered four written questions sent up by a gentleman representing the National Service League. ; One was," Do you consider Lord Roberts to be correct when he states that the invasion of this country is possible ? " To that he replied that there had been no invasion of England since the time of William the Conqueror, and it was not more likely now than it had been for the lasttwo or three hundred years. We pro- fessed to be on friendly terms with other nations. Why should they want to invade us? Mr. King said it could not be too well-remembered that during the seventy years that the State of Peansylvania was carried on on peace principles it alone of all the American. States retained peaceful relations with. the Indian tribes around. As soon as those principles were given up and an army was established the State was engaged In con- I stantetribal wars. The next question was Are there; not nations who would look upon Such an invasion : as the greatest achiovemeat in their history? " I Probably they wonld but *tohat was the motive and who would invade us ? (Applause.) The I 'greatest means of promoting peace was to show ! confidence in the goodwill of other nations. He would like to see this country as an ea: nest of its goodwill, ! make a big reduction in its army and navy and trust to i the other nations following. A third question was : " Does not our unpreparedness tempt nations thus dis- posed and on the other hand wauld not preparation for defence- on the lines indicated by the National Service League render attempts at invasion extre.nely improb- able ? " He thought he had answered thit question alrealy. The last,. question read: " What do the Peace Societies!contemplate in case arbitration failed ?" His answer was that if it did fail we would not be in any worse positioa than we were now. The Rev. C. HARVEY COOK in proposing as vote of thanks to speakers, said they had a Minister for Agricul- ture, and a Minister for War, and he said in all serious- ness they ought to hove a Minister for Peace. (Applause). Until peace was studiedas a science, as an art applied to life, they would not get much further in their principle of peace. (Applause). He advocated that the peace question should be urged solely from the humanitarian point of view. Many estimable Christ- ians countenanced war and in doing so he did not think they were false to their creed. They could appeal to their Bible and point out that Jesus said He came ! not to bring peace beta sword. The Christians who said that Jesus upheld war in that text :were perfectly within their rights in thinking so as we could not say what was in the mind of Jesus. Mr. W. ROBERTS seconded the resolution, and said he was sorry and ashamed that the meeting was not packed to support such a noble cause. The motion was heartily carried, and thanks were then accorded to the Chairman for presiding.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-05_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>OF GREAT INTEREST.</head>
        <p>OF GREAT INTEREST.</p>
        <p>Here is a story of great interest to all those who suffer from backache, dropsy, rheumatism, neuralgia, gout, gravel, stone, sleeplessness, urinary troubles, all of which are the outcome of kidney disease. MIS. H. Tapping, 20, Fenton-street, St. Helens, says :—" Cruel pains in my back troubled me for a long time. One night while I was out walking acold sensation ran down my spine, and my back went crick ; I was so bad that I had to be brought home. I was laid up for some time after that, and the doctor said it was lumbago. My back was so bad that I could not get up and downstairs, aud I was quite unable to bend. It seemed useless for me to take medicines, for the many I tried did not help me at all. My neighbours knew how I suffered. " Hearing that Doan's backache kidney pills were a special kidney medicine, I determined to try them. I found the pills to be just what I required ; they have made me well again, and lam all right now. There is no mistake,Do m's pills are a splendid medicine. (Signed) Harriet Topping." . Doan's Backache Kidney Pills are two shillings and ninepence per box (six boxes for thirteen shillings and ninepeace), Of all chemists and stores, or post free, direct from Foster-McClellan Co., 8, Wells-street, Oxford-street, London, W. Doan's are the pills that cured Mrs. Topping.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-05_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>BEAUTIFUL WA.RRINGTON MOVEMENT. Meeting In St. John's Wad.</head>
        <p>BEAUTIFUL WA.RRINGTON MOVEMENT. Meeting In St. John's Wad.</p>
        <p>A meeting of persons interested in the beautiful War- rington Society was held in the S. Peter's School on Monday evening. There was oaly a small etteniance over which Councillor Fulton S nith presided. The CHalttnav said a geveral idea prevailed that the society was simply for the cultivation of flowers and for the prevention of smoke. The objects want deeper than thr t. They hoped to induce the inhabitants to improve their dwelliries by the cultivation of flowers,to take a greater interest in their households' and to promote tidiness and cleanliness generally. His own idea was that in the winter they should have illustrated lectures about plant life, and other subjects which would be conducive to the advancements of the society. Mr. E. J. HALL hoped the workingmen would take an interest in the society. The northern part of the town needed special attention. It looked as though they could scarcely clothe St. John's and Orford wards with beauty, but he had noeloubt they could in time greatly improve the dietrict and make it much more pleasing to the eye. Mr. JOHN UPSON said it had been charged agairst the Society by the friv,dous that they were ide..lists, but might they not say that the idealists of to-day were the realists of to-morrow. This society had set out to lessen, if not to remove, some of the evils which attended modern industrial life, and surely they ought to expect the support of all good citizens. In St. John's and Orford Wards there were great possibilities. It was difficult in St. John's Ward to fled spots which they might make beautiful, but there were places in Orford where the operations of the Society might be successfully carried on. Much of their energy and means should be devoted to making beautiful the strictly industrial parts of the town, where the people were to a considerable extent helpless, and where their efforts would be the most telling and, probably, in time the most appreciated. The objects of the society commended themselves to the thinking people of the town. The society was one in which all creeds, classes, and schools of thought could work together. They had passed through that period when they were met with a shrug of the shoulder and good humou-, and they were being treated seriously not only by the Press, but others who stood aloof from them at the commencement. He thought they had justified their existence, and in time would show good results. (Applause). Canon STEvENsos urged of the laver:awe of forming ward committees, because, he said, the Central Coin. mitbo COUld not be up sod down every street in the town to see what wanted doing. He was not discouraged by the small attendence that evening. He had implicit faith in the growth of the movement, and no doubt that it would succeed. There VT .4 a great deal to do in St. John's and Orford Wards. St. John's presented perhaps as dillieult problem for beautification as any ward in the town. Consequently it ought to form an early and interesting experiment. That beautifying could be done he had every reason to believe and hose. Orford Ward presented charming possibilities, that ought to be embraced and carried to a successful conclusion. The principles of the society were permeating the chil&amp;en of the schools, and the trees that had been planted were being given a fair chance and had not suffered. If they could succeed in teaching the children the love of the beautiful, this country was going to be afar more beantiful place in the future than it bean in the past. (Applause.) After some discussion it was decided to form one branch for the two wards to commence with, and to have two branches later if sufficient members are forth- coming. On the motion of Mr. W. L. WILLIAMS, Councillor Fulton Smith was elected chairman of the committee. The other members of the committee appointed wore: —Bev. B. Percival, Rev. J. Davies, Rev. J. L. Milne, Dr. Ferguson, Alderman Roberts, Councillor Councillor Stott, Mr. and Mrs. Edgar, n(iats.Ejdogbaar,s, Mr Rose, Miss Rose Messrs. W. Furness. G. Walter, T, S. Steel, J. T. Rose, H. Hayes. Felix Maginn Jos. Bamforth, G. E. Mackey, J. Haviour, W. L. Williams with P. Berry (Orford), and Jos. Hill s as joint secretaries.</p>
      </div>
