      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-05_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>WARRIiifiryTON</head>
        <p>WARRIiifiryTON</p>
        <p>The Coon and Costume Concert. The concert organised by :Mrs. Ralph Johnson last week has realised,4l6 14s. Od., after the payment of expenses, and this sum has been handed over to Miss White, Holly House, Sankey-street, on behalf of the sick and poor and Sunday School Fund of the Parish Church. N.S.P.C.C. Work. The Warrington and District Branch of thel,\Tational Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Children has clarior the quarter ended 31st March, 1900, inquired into 81 complaints of neglect, ill-treatment and other wrongs of childhood, of which all were found to be true, affecting 276 children and 127 offenders. Action was takea as follows : 77 cases were warned, 4 were prosecuted (convicted 4, discharged nil), and nil were otherwise dealt with. The Society's Inspector made 117 visits of supervision.</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>MT DEAR CH/LDREN, I am pleased to say I have received. several interesting letters, telling me how you spent your Easter holidays, but I hope during this week to have many more, in which case I will publish the winners of the prizes next week. You had very nice weather, so lam sure you all enjoyed yourselves. I hope all those who have flower gardens are giving them atten- tion, and keep the young plants watered. Later on I will tell you when to send me the specimen flowers.</p>
        <p>I have to thank many of you for contribu- tions sent me, and the prize this week goes to Minnie Almond, who has tried several limes for a prize. I am sure you will all like the piece of poetry sent by her. I think sit very interesting. I should be pleased to receive more poetry and short stories from you all. I give a prize nearly every week to the best con- tribution sent to the "Children's Corner," so you all stand a good chance of a yrize if you only persevere. Remember, I have offered a prize to the boy and girl sending me the best essay on "Spring." This must be sent to the office of this paper, addressed to me, by May 19. I will say good-bye for this week, so with all good wishes from</p>
        <p>Your affectionate</p>
        <p>UNCLE NED.</p>
        <p>DEAR UNCLE NED,—I am sending you another contribution, entitled</p>
        <p>WHERE THE GREAT RED OWL LIVED.</p>
        <p>Near a great wood there once lived a father and mother, who had one child, a little boy, whose name was Wilson.</p>
        <p>One day, when Wilson had been a very naughty child, the father said to him, "Wilson, if you are not a better boy, I shall have to take you to the great holloiv tree, where the great red owl lives." Now, the father had a beautiful china dish which had been given him by his mother, who received it from her great grandfather, who brought it with him from China, and he valued this dish above all his treasures. Wilson had been forbidden to touch the china dish, but one day, when his father was out, and hie mother was not looking, he climbed up' to the mantel, whereon sat the dish, and picked it up to see what was in it; but even as his hands touched the dish his foot slipped, and he fell, and the treasure was broken into a hun- dred fragments. , That night the fb.ther said: "Wife, there is nothing else to be done; the boy must learn to obey. Wilson put on your little red cap, and kiss your mother good-bye. lam going to take you to the great hollow tree, where the great red owl lives."</p>
        <p>Then the father took Wilson by the hand, and led him past the huge black rock, and .the crooked white brook, and the big red oak, and came to a tree more mighty than any of the others. When they - lop - this ' a queer gruff voice " Who° I whoo I v, "What wa6 that, father?" Wilson asked. "That was the great red owl," the father answered.</p>
        <p>"Whoo I whoo I whoo I" again called the queer gruff voice. "It is naughty Wilson, naughty Wilson," the father answered. "I have brought him to thee." ' _ .</p>
        <p>Then, a door opened in the side of the hollow tree, the father thrust Wilson in through the opening, and the door swung shut. At first. ll was dark, but on looking up Wil- son saw what he took to be two bright lights shining high above his head. "Whoo I whoot whoo I" came the queer gruff voice from the direction of the shining lights. Wilson now saw that the lights were slowly coming ikearer, and growing brighter. Presently it became 5O light that he could see a red ladder leading toward the top of the hollow tree, and the lights seemed to be coming. down this ladder. Nearer and nearer came-the two bright lights, louder and louder sounder the gruff " whoo I whoo I whoo I" of the queer voice, and then with a-hop and the flutter of wings, the great red owl swung himself off the ladder, and stood before Wilson. The bright lights were his two round eyes. " Whoo I whoo I whoo I" said the great red owl, blinking with both eyes at Wilson, and nodding his head slowly. Then, suddenlv, ex- tending one of his huge claws towards Wilson', he said, "Shake bands, naughty Wilson." Wilson held out his hand. The owl gripped it tightly, and began slowly to draw the hand towards him until it was directly under his beak. Then he made a quick peck at the back of the hand. Wilson screamed with fright.</p>
        <p>‘VVVh-oo whool whoo l" e-aid the great owl, "naughty Wilson ha e the mark of the great red owl on his hand. 4" Who! who° I wbool" and he began to hop slowly up the ladder, and soon Wilson was again alone in the darknece at the bottom of the hollow tree. "Whoa! whoo I whoo I" called the great red owl from the top of the long, red ladder. Then Wilson heard something come scurrying %wilily down the ladder, and felt a furry little</p>
        <p>inal climb up his leg, over his waist, and on his shoulder.</p>
        <p>"I will be good! I will be good! Oh, 3 Red Owl, I will be good!" be cried in grl fright: "Whoo I" said the great red owl. Then t .ittle furry animal SI-wilted close to Wilsoi :ar, "Naughty Wilson! Naughty Wilson and scurried off hie shoulder, across his wai down his leg, and up the ladder.</p>
        <p>(To be continued.)</p>
        <p>the )r.'s I!"</p>
        <p>DEAR UNCLE NED,—I am sending you a piece of poetry, and I would very much like to eee it in the paper if you have a space for it. I must close now with best wishes, from yo.;ir loving niece MINNIE ALMOND.</p>
        <p>GOD KNOWS. Oh! wild and dark was the winter night When the "emigrant ship went down, But just outside the harbour bar, In the sight of the startled town. The winds howled, and the spa. roared, And never a soul could sleep— Save the little ones. on their mother's breast, Too young to watch and weep. No boat could live in the angry surf, No rope could reach the laud; There were bold brave hearts upon the shore, There was many a ready hand: Women who prayed, and men who strove, When their prayers and work were vain; F‘or the sun rose over the awful void And the silence of the main. All day the watchers paced the sands, All day they scanned the deep, All night the booming minute guris Echoed from steep to steep. • "Give up thy dead, 0 cruel sea," They cried athwart the . space; But only a baby's fragile form Escaped from its stern embrace. Only one little child of all • Who with the ship went down That night when the happy babies slept So warm in the sheltered town. Wrapped in the glow of tqx..e morning light, It lay on the shifting sand, ' As fair as a sculptor's marble dream, With a shell in its dimpled hand. There was none s tep of its race or kin; "God knoweth," the pastor said, When the sobbing children -crowded to ask The name of tile baby dead. And so when they laid it away at ,last In the ch"rehyard's hushed repose, They raiced a stone at .the baby's head, With the CZTVCD words—" GOD KNOWS "</p>
        <p>CURiOUS GLASS. An Englishman who was just returning from Africa brought home with him a negro as his servant. ,The boy had always been used to hot countries, and had never seen water in its solid form as ice. One morning he came into his master's room carrying a large piece of ice, and • exclaiming, " See, master, what a large piece of glass I have found." His master, by say of a joke, told lim to put it in the oven to dry. Very soon he at back carrying the partly melted ice, Lnd saying, "See, master, this is the-queerest glass I ever saw; the more I dry it the wetter it gets."</p>
        <p>FANNIE LANGLEY.</p>
