        <p>1 ' •</p>
        <p>The growth of the use of the bicycle for military purposes is a fact to which less attention is given than its importance deserves, It is recalled forcibly to mind, however, by a statement issued by ,the New Hudson Cycle Co., to the effect that since last summer they have supplied exceeding 5,000 bicycles for the) use of cyclist sections of the British army at home and abroad. As the New Hudson Co. is only one of several firms supplying military' cycles to the British Government, the total number of cycles in use for military purposes must be enormous. It is worth while rememberinc, that twelve years ago there was, not a single bicycle in use for military purposes, and that Colonel A. R. Savile, ti4e originator of the idea of military cyoling, is still a cyclist and an active member of the famous Stanley Cycling Club. The Handy Lady's Bicycle.</p>
        <p>The lightm and handiness of the modern lad-3''s bicycle is a constant source of wonder to ladis who do not cycle. A little while back, being in North Wales for a short holiday, the writer witnessed a scene which appeared to strike some lady bystanders ivith astonishment The road led across a wide shallow stretch (If the Dee, but a detour of a couple of miles had to be made in a general way by all wheeled traffic, while the more venturesome foot passengers where able to cross by stepping stones which saved that long detour. A couple of lady cyclists arrived at the edge of the stream, hesitated a moment, then each picking up her bicycle carried it safely across by means of the 'stepping stones, arriving at the other side dry foot and safely, Not much in that, perhaps, but it seemed like a perfect object lesson upon the light- ness and handiness of the modevi lady's bicycle. • -</p>
        <p>'OSTMAN RECORD THAT SPEAKS VOLUME, few words I did qualii</p>
        <p>WILL BE FOUND ON PAGE 44 OF THE NEW HUDSON 1906 CYCLE CATALOGUE, which says: Mr. P. FRANKLIN, of Ivy Cottage, Shipton-undor-Wyehwood, is a postn2as who writes Ifs that he has during the past 8 years ridden his Mew MIDSON BICYCLE 8 miles every day, or a total of 21,684 miles carrying each day about 60 lbs., or a.totai.of 149,760 ihs._,_ and the</p>
        <p>bicycle still runs splendidly. , That is a record for endurance. Mr. G. OLLEY rode one of these bicycles from Land's End to John if ' Itroats in 3 day. 20 hours 15 minwtes without a single mishap—not even a</p>
        <p>puncture. That is a record for speed.</p>
        <p>PROPoSED POLICE STATION'. Superintendent Strickland, ut the County Police,. Wrote :—lZeferring to your letter dated 2nd November, Ikos, re .yostir.Couhcil's viieu for en additional constable and the provision of a lock-up, I have given the matter careful considerritken and have come to the conclusion 'Nit the time is not yet opportune for applitT for more pike. As regards the provision of a lot k-up, I think one is much-needed at Sankey end I sin !worn- mecrling that a pollee station be built :A Sankey. If .1:y recommendation is carried out, the sergeant will livt, in the new police station, thus distributing the police more venly over the district. If built the police atic ion n ill, of course, contain cells and 1 think LUIS will also have a good effect in the 'district. The Chairman expres,ed pleasure at the Soperintee- dent's reply, pointing out that .the Council had been agitating for the provision of a police station for at 'least three years. He had no doubt that iota few )ears they wouN have a Court held at Sankey and that prisoners itould be tried tbero inat ci 01 using con- ve)ed to Warrington. Lie also hopod that before long hey wOidd have a inagistnito in th 3 dist ric`.• Mr. Furness reniarkecl that it slemld be understood that the extra police provision u;is at, required for .esidents but for uudairaiges who cadio into the parish. kLaughaeri. In • answer to Mr. Farness, the Chairman said he believed it was proposed to t root the police statiou near the church. The COO of the bullding would hardly effect them locally. It was decided that the •Clerk should write to Super- intendent Stricklaud expmsing the Council's approval oI the proposal. After 1301110 discussion the Chairman undertook to suggest to the Superintendent the desirability of pro- viding a mortuary in the new building. PnauosEn READ:LNG 1100NL The Ciiairman said it had been thought desirable that they should have a publicpreiiding room for the use of the young men of the district. lie believed the Vicar had undertaken to provide it room and the next step would be to form a committee. Mr. Furness said he bed spoken to several residents who thoroughly approved of the scheme. He, however, hardly felt disposed to push the matter further until thee were assured of a greater interest being displayed in che project by those outside the Council. It was too late in the season to open a rending room now. It was decided to defer the mater for three months.</p>
        <p>SUGGESTED RECREATION GROUND. The Chairman s+titi he had approubial Colonel Ridg- way with reference to a piece of laud in flood-lane it was thought woulod be suitable for a recreation g round. tie found, however, that the Colonel intended to bad on it. If tho Council had to procure a plot of 'aid and fence it recital, it would b.., a custly under- taking. . The qu,,stion was arijoin•becl iadefinitely. TILE DUST NUISANCE. The Chairrinin said they were all puzzled'as to what wee the best course to take, with regard to the dust caused by the mutorE,hut he thought the Cotrinil ought it, psss a resolution calling on the District• Council to take up the matter. _ . . .</p>
        <p>Mr. 'Furness thought the fault lay entirely. in the etheds of constructing the roads which In some nstances were most imonitary. The Chair:eau said 04 dust caused dam“ge to farmers owners of property and tenants. Fie thought the method of construction of the cars was largely responsible for ,the Enhance. Mr. Birch thought the speed was the chief factor. The Chairman : it is a very serious matter for many people. Mr. fliri,h : I don't see any way a getting out of it. The Chain:An : There must he some way. it is impossilde to go on as we are doing. Mr. iiiggs thought the nui&amp;7ance Was greater on the Liverpool-load thin on any other highway. The dust rose just litea field on II to. Mr. Birch said the nukat.ce was equally bad on the Mt rinchain-rer d. The Chairaten said thst when bicycles were first brought out, it wee till unanimous opinion that the , Liverpool-nod mvs one of the finest in the country for 'cycling. Now, however, the road wns not good enough for the her,v3- trati:c-wl,b,b gassed over it. Me.fligvio It 13 the wide tyres that Call6C mischief ' Mr. Btr.h iam afraid we shall have to icve the nitittir to the Ilorte•of, t oman cttsor the lionselof Lords.</p>
        <p>The Chairman : d certainly shall not let the, matter rest, but tball do all I Can to., bring ahout an alteration. Mr. Birch said that at ktattsf.arl the nuisance had been st-ct eeefully dealt eith, ltd. their Snrceyer Newcomlie) said the method ttins a sery expulsive One, and that in bad ,teatter the road soon became slippery. Mr. Furness suggested that the Chairman should bring pressure to bc4r ou the District Couacil to adopt some n.t.;ts.tre by ichich the reads could he rharle of a mbre suEd rles.ctiption. Insiead of utilizing the Itteepings Iron, the road to reconstruct the highways they should use something eta more healthy and sanitary character. The Chairman said the County Council had to deal with the hilt ?cads. The matter dropped without any course of action being agreed to.</p>
        <p>SORE PROTECTIVE VILIIE • in that it would be likely to attract bird as a point at which to' strike. If a bird, when chas- ing a butterfly or moth, were to pierce one of its eye-spots, little damag9 would be done to ; the insect, which would gala, time to evade its pur- suer. On the other hand, the fate of an insect would be sealed if a bird once struck at and in- jured `. its body. This suggestion was first made by Darwin. Moreover, in the ease of solitary, staring eye-spots, such as those seen on the wings of the South American ' owl ' butterflies and many moths, the utility is probably to scare away inquisitive birds by giving the rest- ing insect some resemblance to the head of a terrible monster. In the case of moths not a few of the ,ocelli occupy isuch a position upon the tore wings ,that they/ are brought into par- ticular, prominence when the insect hangs wait- ing for itS wings to dry and• expand after leav- ing the cocoon. This is, of course, -the most critical period of the moth's career; and any mark .or device calculated to scare away enemies becomes extremely valuable. In conclusion, it may be said that the eye-spot is a most striking instance of the manner in which Nature applies a beautiful ornament to the exigencies of brute life, answering by one effort her demands for beauty and utility."</p>
