      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-05_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <p>Loose Handle-ends. It frequently happens that after some use the grips on a handle:bar become loose, and defy all</p>
        <p>'attempts to sticure them by means of dontestic adhesives, like elastic glue, &amp;c. The remedy is quite simple and inexpensive. Bluemel's cold cement, sold in tubes, used according to directions, fixes the loose grip once and for all. The Renewal of Brake Blocks. Now that every one uses a free wheel, the maintenance of brakes assumes paramount import- ance. The renewal of brake blocks may be deferred if, after wear has manifested itself, the blocks be taken out of the shoes . and reversed. The writer has found this answer on crescent rims. New blocks may be bought of almost any cycle-1 agent for a few pence, and if not of the exact pattern required, they may be cut to fit the shoes with the aid of a penknife. The job should be undertaken in a leisurely manner, as nicety of fitting is essential to success. In Most cases the shoes of the brake are detachable. On their removal, the old block is easily pushed out, and a new one can then be fitted. Before riding the machine, careful inspection should be made to ascertain that the brake clears the rim when "off," and that there is no risk of the . blocks fouling the valve, when the, brake is applied. It may be necessary to pare the blocks to ensure perfect action.</p>
