      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-05_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>EYESIGHT AND ELECTRIC LIGHT.</head>
        <p>EYESIGHT AND ELECTRIC LIGHT.</p>
        <p>A great eye speciali:lt declares that electric light is less damaging to the eyes than other forms of artificial light. Taking as a basis of de- drotion the numly2r of times a patient closed the Hs of his eyes, he found that the lids closed fi*,3 times in a miniit,) with candle light, 2.8 times with gas light. 2.2 times with sun light, and I'B times wth electric light.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-05_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>AMA ZING SPEED.</head>
        <p>AMA ZING SPEED.</p>
        <p>We often read of scot-chin, motorists bein7 hauled before the magistrates and fined for driv- ing at the terrific rate" of forty miles per hour. Compared with the velocity of other mov- ing objects with Which science. deals, such as X-rays and radium emanations, this speed is very insignificant. The Beat-ray emanations from radium are shot out at the • inconceivable speed of 100,000 miles per second, 300 times that bf the most swiftly moving star, 200,000 times that of a rifle bullet, and 4,500,000 times that of the quickest express train on our railways. The mind refuses to grasp these figures, but they are, by far and away, the quickest moving objects in the universe, and have such amazing penetration that 12in. of solid iron is required to intercept them. They are, unlike light and sound, not merely waves or undulations, but palpable, ma- terial particles of matter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-05_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>CHEMICO-LUMINESCENCE.</head>
        <p>CHEMICO-LUMINESCENCE.</p>
        <p>Some curious experiments on what he calls chemico-luminescence. are recorded by Profepor Trautz. He found that most brilliant lumin- escence was caused by the mixture at 'a low tem- perature of peroxide of hydrogen with pyrogallol and formaldehyde, and that it 'gave a continuous spectrum from the red to the blue-green, with the maximum effect in the orange-red. :Liaising the temperature of the mixture seemed to in- crease the light, but the rays were cut off by the interposition of a sheet of aluminium of Emm. in thickness. A mixture of chloride of calcium. finely powdered with hot chloric, acid also gave a brilliant effect. One would be curi- ous to know if either of these mixtures proved itself capable of ionizing the . surrounding air. and, if so. whether it was in circumstances which support Professor Rutherford's theory that ioni- zation is in many cases the effect of phosphor-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-05_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>eseence.</head>
        <p>eseence.</p>
        <p>-THE EYE-SPOT IN NATURE.</p>
        <p>" The Eye-Spot in Nature" is the 'subject of an interesting article in Pearsan's Magazine, in which many fascinating details are given on one of the ' most curious phenomena in Nature. Th. 6 forms—on the. feathers eye-spot appears in many of a peacock, on the wings of moths and butter- flies, on the skins of many wild beasts, and the C•loraces of many insects. The difficulty has always . been to explain its purpose. •. Mr. Percy Collins, the author of the article.. writes as follows: By far the .most numermis recurrences of the eye-spot in the insect world are seen upon the wings of butterflies and moths. In some instances the distinctive mark is small and oft repeated : in otlirs it is large, solitary, and staring. • Moreover the collar and 'make- up ' varies as, much as its size, the most curious variety being the eye-spot with a perfectly trans- parent centre. 'Now such very striking and com- plicated markings cannot become characteristic of large families of butterflies and moths without some important meaning attaching to the cir- cumstance.; and it has been suggested as possible " that the eye possesses •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-05_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>CAN WE 'USE RADIUM?</head>
        <p>CAN WE 'USE RADIUM?</p>
        <p>Is ;radium ever likely to be of any practical use? It may, or it may not, light our lamps or drive our motors. The assured certainty is, says the Scientific American, that if it is ever made to do either of these things, man 04 Ite know him will quickly follow his steam engine and dynamo into the museum. At present we have not found how to control the gigantic forces., or to affect the tremendous processes we have so lately dis- covered, and we ' never may. The important point is that by these discoveries the relations of mankind to Nature have undergone a certain change, and he has easight a• glimPse of some latent possibilities within his legitimate destiny which cannot be effaced. Energy is the life of the physical universe. You cannot multiply the existing store by mi llion and leave things as they were. But the recognition of the internal energy of matter has done this xvithin the pro- gress of the century which has just begun. A journey to , the moon to-morrow may rank with the half-penny car ride of to-day,, or the warm- ing of a house with the thawing of an Arctic continent. To-day, so far as any use of the new knowledge is concerned, the one is as impossible as the other, but notie would, venture to 'deny to the future the possibility.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-05_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>PRIMITIVE MAN ON THE SAHARA.</head>
        <p>PRIMITIVE MAN ON THE SAHARA.</p>
        <p>It is surprising 'to find the Sahara largely grass-lands, -tenated with , animal life, but more remarkable to know that it ;was formerly popu- lous. • Gautier found absolute proof that long • before the present age of rainfall, in what is known as the Neolithic or later Stone Age, a very large population inhabited this part of .the Sahara. He found there graves scattered over the grassy plain; he found many hundreds of their drawings on the rocks, where they had pictured animal forms and other objects. He discovered the flattened stones which, they had used for grinding grain. These millstones show that agriculture was then . developed in that re- gion, and the grinding of grain into flour indi- cates considerable advance of civilisation. Here ' and there *ere many .arrow-poipts, •- axes of polished stone, and other implements. It was many hundreds of years ago that human being inhabited this region, but, as time is reckoned in geological epochs, thousands of farmers were tilling this part'of the Sahara at a comparatively • recent (period. .They were finally driven baclf" into the Soudan by the increasing 'drought\ and ; the world forgot that this region had ever been ; inhabited by man. I</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-05_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>ARE HE PLANETS HABITABLE?</head>
        <p>ARE HE PLANETS HABITABLE?</p>
        <p>- Great additions have been made of late years to. our knowledge of the laws of radiation of heat and light. Tha•-, has Made it pOssible to cal- culate with *some confidence the temperature of the sun, and also the temperatures of . the planets. It is now pretty well ascertained, says the Hon. R. J. Strutt, F.R.S., that the sun's temperature is about 6,CCOdeg. Centigrade, or about 3.1,000 deg. Fahrenheit. Perhaps it may be said that this is somewhat less than double the temperature of the, electric furnace, .the Ino.sL Powerful means of heating which we posses. An Oven snore interesting \inquiry is the</p>
        <p>TEUPERATURE OF THE PLANETS. For on that most largely depend the possibilitY of their being the abode of living creatures. In the case of the planets. everything de- pends on the presence or absence of an atmo- sphere. The earth's atmosphere has a regulating ofTca ; in he day - tithe it protects the surface from the full intensity of the sun's heat, while at night it acts as 'a blanket, preventing the sudden loss of the stock of heat acquired in the clay-time. 'We do not know enough about the etinosphere .of Venus and Mars to say definitely whether or riot the temperatures of those planets are regulated in a similar way. But let us as- sume that such is the case. Then it wou!d appear from calculations that the average tem- perature of Venus would be about 125 deg. Fahrenheit—something like the temperature to be met with in the stokehold of a P. and 0. steamer in the Red Sea in summer-time. Mars, on the other hand, must be</p>
        <p>iurcn COLDER THAN TIIE EARTH. In fact, the average temperature there would be such as just ahont, to freeze the mercury in th 7 thermom..ter: a phcnomenon within the expel' once of Arctic explorers, and, therefore, by tic, means excluding the possibility of life. Mars has white polar caps which are observed to increase during the Mattia,n winter, and to diminish in he Martian summer. If these -.vhite ratchet consist ot 5110 W and ice. whicli seems in i any respects the most natural view, the ealcula;ion of temperature must be altogether at fault, for in that case the temperature over moat of the -..-J.an.et must be above the freezingipoiut of water.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-05_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>CRICKET.</head>
        <p>CRICKET.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON V. BLACKLEY.—Tho opening match of the season was played at Arpley on Saturday. A cold north-easter prevailing during the afternoon, a blinding hailstorm interfered with the play. Blackley batted first on a wicket which Alia on the slow side, and afforded the howlers no little assistance. Cresswell and Thewlis were the truadiers, and the Ii °nour of taking the first wicket of the season fell to the professional, who got i Knowles caught by Callon. The Blackley professional j played steady cricket until 'Richardson held him safely. Wild had only a short stay, Cresswell bowling him with a break-back. Radford and Phillips were the only bate- men to make anything like a stand, both getting into double figures. Smart fielding caused .11adford's and Moore's retirement. The attack always prevailed, and the innings closed for 55. Soon after reaching the pavilion there was another hailstorm, followed by rain, and play was subsequently abandoned. Scores :</p>
        <p>BLACKLEY. J. Knowles c Callon b Cresswell 8 T rylor c Richardson b Thewlis J. 'Wild b Cresswell J. T. Brine c Thewlis b Creswell 1 E. Radford run out 1 W. Phillips bThewliB S. Morres run out G. Tattersall b Thewlis 0 R. C. Grant tkThompson b Thewlis 2 W. Ashton not out A. Parkinson b Creswell Extras 2</p>
        <p>Total</p>
        <p>55</p>
        <p>BOWLING ANALYSIS.</p>
        <p>0. R. M. W. Creswell 12.5 20 3 4 Thewli</p>
      </div>
