      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-05_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>LETIIRS TO THE EDITOR.</head>
        <p>LETIIRS TO THE EDITOR.</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS. Authors of letters intended for publication must in all cases send their names and addresses to the Editor as a guarantee of good faith.</p>
        <p>" And it Game to Pass." thank your correspondent, Mr. L. Weßings, for his letter. In reply, I beg to say that my article was intended to convey the fact that, in future, I shall do all that I possibly can to further the aims and objects—ideals, if yon will—of Socialism. Official Liberalism does not stand for Socialism, so I have resigned my membership of the Party. I hope my action may not be misconstrued. We shall see.—Yours truly, EDMUND OSBORNE. 29, Lovely-lane, Warrington, May 3, 1906.</p>
        <p>Mr. Jolley and the Education Committee</p>
        <p>Sir.—l am glad to see the representative element in the Council on Tuesday last showed a majority for what is undoubtedly the popular idea, i.e. the referring back of :qr. Jolley's name to the Education Committee. The contrary was carried by the aldermanic vote, which as usual went against the popular cause. We shall soon have to consider the necessity of doing away with unrepresentative" representatives," whether co-opted in the Education Committee or aldermanic in the Council. I hope Mr. Jolley is satisfied, but I still subscribe myself, yours Sm.,</p>
        <p>MARK TAPLEY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-05_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>As sociation Football.</head>
        <p>As sociation Football.</p>
        <p>Sir,—lf the two letters of Mr. T. P. Bradshaw, under this heading, are to be taken as examples of the domi- neering attitude of the League towards its members one is not altogether surprised that association football in Warrington has not prospered. After reading them one is not inclined to ask whether he is the servant of the committee or whether the committee is his catspaw ? I asked Mr. Bradshaw two questions which he has condescended to answsr; not because lasked him, but pro bone publico. My first was " Row many outsiders were fed at the officials expense," and his answer is " Six with representatives of the press." My second was" Whether two members of the Executive Com- ..</p>
        <p>mitteo have been appointed auditors, as, according to rule 22, all accounts above £2 shall be brought before the Executive, and if approved, he paid." He surely does not think that it is quite in order for the aacounts , to be audited by two members of the Committee which ' has the spending of the money'?" His reply comes " Yes." Now, Sir, the Warrington and District Football Association is or should be a public institution, and, as such the accounts should be presented to the public and I ask him to be good enough to publish the accounts so that they may be seen by the public. Yours truly. FULL BACK".</p>
        <p>Militarism, Sir,—ln the absence abroad of my friend Captain Crosfield perhaps you will allow me to say a few words with reference to the recent meeting held in Warrington on the subject of Militarism. Everyone must be in sympathy with the objects and aims of the so-called Peace Societies. The only point on which we differ is as to the best means to be adopted to gain these ends. lam sorry the speakers at the meeting had such a poor audience, as even when I don't agree with a man I like him to have every opportunity of being listened to. A man who agrees with me because he has not heard the other side is of little use to himself or anybody else. The person I want is he who after having heard both sides and exercised his own judgment then joins forces with me. Mr. Holmes appears to think that the main object of The National Service League is to create a sufficiently dense atmosphere of dlnamite and gunpowder, amidst which the awash buckling members may buccaneer to their hearts' content. Now if the conveners of this meeting appreciated the principles of the National Service League a little more thoroughly we should have no more hearty supporters than the members of the different Peace Societies throughout the kingdom. Mr. A. J. King has had a business training. With regret let me add, I voted for him at the last election. That buiness training of his ought to allow him to realize that if we are to succeed as a business nation in the .quiet possession of our trade and our territories it is abso- lutely essential that we should be safe from interference from outside. With all respect to the "cheek-turning" theory, this is hardly a practical doctrine for our modern life of to-day, and the nation that is least likely to be attacked is the one which is best known to be capable of defending itself. Personally I have joined the National Service League because with my other interests I have no particular desire to have to undertal:e the active defence of my country,and because were its principles universally adopted I know there would be little chance of my being called away from my own fire side. It is just because I have not got that feeling of security that I want everyone to be in a position to do his share if necessary. The answers given to questions raised at the end of Mr. King's speech hardly bear logical inquiry. As regards the possibility of invasion, the value of an opinion largely depends on the identity of the person giving it, and Lord Roberts is admittedly an authority. With reference to its being necessary to take some risk, a similar assumption would be that it is absurd to lock one's front door at night or even have bolts on the windows because there have been no burglaries in my road for the last ten years, and why on earth should any one wish to take something which does not belong to them. Personally I prefer to put the latch down and sleep soundly in consequence. Let every lad be taught the responsibilities of citizen- ship and that duty to his country comes before aoght else ; let him experince good, sound, wholesome dis- cipline on the right lines and we shall have clean,strong men to follow ; bring home the fact that the desire of the individual must be subordinated to the welfare of the Society, and you will have less loafing and less inefficiency. That the main principles of the League will in the end prevail I have no doubt. May I say with all reverence Ged grant it may not be too late.— Yours faithfully, Warrington, May 2nd, 190 G.</p>
        <p>HOL3IAN KINGDON.</p>
        <p>.• . ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-05_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>Workers and Education.</head>
        <p>The Workers and Education.</p>
        <p>Sir,--Someone has said "If there is one thing in the world that is terrible, it is the tyranny of dogmatism. Nothing is so dangerous AS a theologian in power." It is this tyranny of dogmatism that is the cause of the pitiful controversy now going on between two sections of the Christian Church. On the one hand we have the Conformists who contend for denominational instruc- tion in elementary schools and on the other hand we have the Nonconformists who desire the elementary schools to be freed from denominational teaching altogether. Denominationalism is of course synonymous with dogmatism and the tyranny of dogmatism is a thing which all broad-minded and thinking people should avoid. Both the Conformists and the] Nonconformists have the same ultimate aim, namely to save people from the destructive forces of hell and prepare them for participation in the joys of future bliss. Theologians do not agree upon the nature of Hell or Heaven. Some believe in eternal punishment; some do not. They are not in agreement as to the nature of Heaven ; all is speculative and yet it is for the teaching of speculative doctrines that men are prepared to forget the divine command of love, charity and affection one towards another. There are oci the other hand principles of life and conduct which are common • to all sections of the Christian Church and if these principles are taught in our elementary day schools and if the Government sanctions the teaching of these principles during school hours it is fulfilling all the obligations religious and moral which can be laid upon it as the head of a Christian State. The handling of the question of Education by the Clerics has been thri means of bringing about the present confusion. There seems to be now only one solution and that solution is only possible when those who are most affected by Elementary Education awake to their responsibilities and seize the opportunity which just now lies within their grasp. The Labour Party as the direct representatives of the class of people referred to have also a great responsibility in this matter. It is their duty to give this question of education their first and most serious attention. Education must be placed before Trades Union claims, Housing, Temper- ance, Land Iteforna, 3:c. The workinp, class have the destiny of this England of ours in their own hands and a reasonable, stern and unflinching demand from democracy is bound to be obeyed. Why are the poor., poor? Why are they content in a large degree with things of low estate? Is it not to a large degree faulty education, a false idea of their own power, and perhaps an imperfect idea of Christianity ? It has undoubtedly been the policy of the landlords to d,.ny education to the masses. It was, and is now to their7advantage that they (the masses) should know so much and no more; that they should be forced to act as machines and loose their individuality. What is wanted at the noment time is a demand from the masses for more genuine consideration in the matter of educa- tion, and the demand must be made in no uncertain way through their true representatives, and nct through any religious organisation, any priests, clergyman or Nonconformist parson in their capacity as ministers of religion. Whv should they monopolise this question in the way they do ? Church of England clergymen have less right to interfere in this matter than any one else. Many of them receive their livings at the gift of the landlords and they must and do act for and on behalf of the landlords in this matter of Educa- tion. Democracy and antocracy are opposed to one another and workingmen would do well to remember that the Clergy, with a few notable exceptions, are hand in glove with autocracy; receiving their livings from them. One is forced to the conclusion that the denominationalists are jealous of the growth of Non- conformity and in order to retain their bold are pre- pared to forget their Christianity in order to gain a</p>
        <p>111110001</p>
        <p>Pnpremacy in numbers over their opponents. They wish to make the children converts to their own par- ticular dogmas. They care very little about the children's material welfare so long as they can count them among their flock in order to swell their numbers. Then again what are their dogmas ? Is the belief in any particular creed going to make a man a better Christian? Will a Roman Catholic priest please say 'bluntly that all those outside the Roman Catholic body are eternally damned. Dare the Church of England clergyman say that all those who do not accept the thirty nine articles are to be shut out of Heaven ? Dare any Nonconformist body claim the sole right to Christ's Kingdom. If not, then why attach so much importance to these things ? Is the doctrine of the Brotherhood of man confined to any particular soots ? is honesty, is truth, is love, is charity or kindness? and if you take these away from Christianity what have you left? l'he English workingman has the power to settle this question. Let him stand on his dignity and deny the right of the clergy to say what his children must be taught, Let the voice of the nevi° be heard through their own representatives in Parliament. May I appeal to the workingmen to stand firm for the teaching of those principles which will lead to right and virtuous living. To rid their minds of the importance of dogma and insist upon an education that will lead their children in after years to recognise the brotherhood of man and perform such acts which will show a sincere desire to promote love, charity and kindness among their fellows. Working- men will do well to organise their forces, hold meet- ings, sign petitions, and above all resist any clerical interference. Say in no uncertain way that they do not wish their children to be educated in dogma but in life's principles, principles which will make life's struggle easier. Dogmas can be left alone until children can solve these speculative doctrines for them- selves.—Yours faithfully, "LIBERTY." Stockton Heath. May 2nd.</p>
      </div>
