      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-05_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS. RUGBY FOOTBALL. By " Ovalist "</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS. RUGBY FOOTBALL. By " Ovalist "</p>
        <p>Nry review of the season will be fount! in another column.</p>
        <p>Warrington finished up the season with a rattle and Hunslet soon saw their championship chances vanish- ing like a beautiful dream. They came with a clear deter- mination to break if possible their run of ill-luck on the Warrington ground, having the additional induce- ment of knowing that success might secure them the League Championship unless Leigh wow at Barrow. Leigh only succeeded in making a draw, but as Hunslet lost by 15 points to 2 points it did not matter in the least except to ilunslet. The Warrington men did Leigh a good turn, and I hope the Mather Laners will not be unmindful of the fact.</p>
        <p>For a team with their record Hunslet gave a very poor show at Wilderspool. They were fully repre- sented, and possessed physique enough to impress most people, but they never displayed a vestige of champion form. Perhaps the reason was that War- rington were at their best, and gave the Hunslet men no quarter in a very fast gains. The general expecta- tion was that Warrington would suffer from the effects of a fortnight's idleness, but the contrary was the caso. They made the pace a cracker and kept it up to the close. Both forwards and backs were in fine trim and quite value for the 15 points they obtained. In com- parison Hnnslet were very slow and uncertain in their methods, and scarcely ever seemed likely to score. Though ten points behind in the first half they plodded away to the end, the only consolation— and a very small one—being a dropped goal from the pro- ficient foot of Albert Goldthorpe.</p>
        <p>On the Warrington side, Fish gave a display which made many people wish the season was just beginning instead of ending. We have not seen much of him this season owing to injury, but on Saturday he played with all his old brilliance, dash and confidence. Of late be has seemed short of pace, but he surprised everyone when he kicked over the full back's head and simply raced away from all pursuers to score a try on Saturday. The question of speed had just been the subject of discussion on the stand when Fish got clear and promptly settled the argument. There was no one in the Hunslet side who could come near him. Lees and Brooks did well at half, and the former opened his scoring account for Warrington with a dropped goal and a try. Taylor, Dickenson, Jordan and Berry all finished up well, while Harmer and Heath were again prominent in the loose</p>
        <p>On the Hunslet side the two Goldthorpes were the most prominent, notwit,hstanding the years they have played football. Albert was exceptionally resource- ful and energetic and I feel rather sorry that in thi j s —probably his last season—he has only just failed to lead his team to the highest honours. He is of the type of footballer that all can admire, and probably never had his superior as a dropper of goals. The one he popped over on Saturday was quite masterly. There was no apparent preparation —though it was a penalty kick just a quick half turn, a slurp kick,and the ball passed neatly through the uprights. It is a good thing for other teams that dropped goals only count 2 points, though they have sufriced to win Hunslet many a match as it is. •Like their skipper, Hunslet took their licking in very good part and proved sportsmanlike losers. We don't want them to w;ii at Warrington, but better luck next time when there is a championship within reach!</p>
        <p>Leigh, of course, secured the Championship, though they were lucky to halve the points at Barrow. It was not a very brilliant wind-up, but some account must be taken of the absence of men like Dunbavin, Clarkson and Bennett.</p>
        <p>As I foretold, Salford failed for the fourth time to get thrsngb the final tie for the Northern Union Cup. They were only beaten by 5 points to nil, but Bradford were clearly the better side and deserved their success. Few people outside Salford had much confidence in the Reds, hut their turn will come some day,l suppose. Cycling. The weather on Saturday was anything but ideal for cycling, rain and hail falling during a great part of the afternoon, and the result was that only about a dozen members from each club turned out for the Warrington Club's inter-club run to Hoc) Green with the Manchester Wheelers. On arrival at the Kilton a few of the hardier spirits indulged in several games of bowls but the sport was spoiled by thel wintry conditions. After tea there was an impromptu concert and this over the wheelers left for home, the outing having been a pleasant if rather chilly experience. Cricket.</p>
        <p>The works competition has been successfully inaugurated and promises to be taken up with great spirit by the cricket enthusiasts at the various local places of industry. There was a very representative attendance at tha meeting held at the Arpley Pavilion on Monday evening, go fewer than twenty clubs send- ing delegates. With shch a large entry the competition will be ork ed on the "knock- out principle and there should be plenty of excitement. Each side will bat fifty minutes, but in case of rain or bad light the match will be finished at a date to be arranged by the committee which has been appointed. The opening match wilt take place next Friday evening. Entries close on Monday, and tho draw will take place the same evening.</p>
        <p>The Moore and Daresbnry Club open their season to-day (Saturday) with a match with Lymnrat home. An excellent list of fixtures has been arranged and a sue- cesiful season is anticipated. The President of the Club is Sir Gilbert Greenall Bart ; captain, Rev. T. T. Lee Jones, M.A. ; vice-captain, Mr. S. Taylor ; hon. treasurer, Rev. J. S. Barnes, M.A. ; hon. secretary, Mr. R. Percy Booth, Darosbury. The ground is at Moore Park.</p>
        <p>VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE IS GUARANTEED TO CURE A COUGH OR COLD IN ONE NIGHT. The romance of the rise of VIVO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE is most remarkable. In seven years this famous remedy has won its way into nearly every home in Great Britain and the 'Colonies. Upwards of two million bottles are sold annually. Doctors prescribe it because it is absolutely the safest, purest, and most efficient remedy procurable for : INFLUENZA ASTHMA CATARRH WEAK LUNGS CHRONIC COUGHS CROUP BRONCHITIS WHOOPING COUGH ALBERT SMITH, M.S.B.Sc., Analytical and Bacteriological Laboratory, 30, Sotheby-road, ,FlighbuTy Park, London' N., in his certificate of analysis says :--- find VENG'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE Com- posed of pure ingredients carefully compounded, whose combined effect Is to allay irritation and facilitate the ejection of mucus, Booth and stimulate the lungs, thus enabling any lesion of the respiratory tract to heal, and so preventing that distressing irritation and constant coughing which produces exhaustion. In my opinion it is a most excellent remedy, and can be relied upon as safe and effective medicine in asthma, bronchitis and all affections of the lungs for which I thoroughly recommend it."</p>
        <p>--isii-KTir.E-17170'S LIGHTING COUGH CURE, at Chemists everywhere. Price 91d., Is. IA. and 2s. 9d.</p>
