        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, May 5, 1906.</p>
        <p>Committee would guarantee the rent of the cottages, tone of voice which could not be heard by those who I that it would be an excellent thing for Mr. Henshall to were passing by. But Mr. Henshall received extra- buy the land and erect the cotta ordinary corroboration in the case from the stir- guarantee from the Fire Committee, that he (Mr. ges if he could get a rounding circumstances. It would have been impos- Peacock) was on the Fire Committee and the Finance sible for him to have had the knowledge which he Committee and there need be no fear about the thing possessed when he went before the committee as to through, and that he (Mr. Peacock) was to going throu what had gone on in the Finance and Fire Engine Corn- receive £lOO out of it when the thing did go through. mittees unless it had been communicated to ?him by Mr. Henshall discussed the matter with him and some member of the committees. It would not be sug- inquired why he(Mr.Peacock)did not take the matter;up gested that he had ever been in conversation with any himself if it was such a good thing,and defendant said it other member but Mr. Peacock and it was admitted was not convenient to him to do so at that time. Imatedi- that Mr. Peacock had a conversation with him about ately on reaching Warrington they went together to the matter. The story was also corroborated by the view the land in Flag-lane Mr. Peacock pointed attitude which Mr. Peacock himself took up all through. out to Mr. Henshall, who was a bit dubious about the He was always anxious that the matter should be kept matter, the way in which the cottages could be erected, open in order that Mr. Henshall should have an oppor- and ultimately they parted with a rather significant tunity of coming forward and of building the cottages. remark from Mr. Peacock " Now remember if you He (Mr. Swift) submitted that in a case of that kind it : w hand this over to anyone else and I don't get my £loo,as impossible to find a storymore clearly corroborated I shall block it and it won't come off at all. Mr. by the surrounding circumstances than the one which Henshall went away and he would tell the bench that Mr. Henshall would relate to the Bench. he did nothing else in the matter. He did not like the idea—he thought there Was something " fishy" about ' THE EVIDENCE. it. He did not approach Mr. Peacock again and he did not approach the Corporation in any way to acquire the land and the matter remained in that position until January 23rd when it again came before the Finance Committee. It was significant that at that meeting Mr. Peacock asked whether any application bad been made by Mr. Henshall for the land and he was told that there had not. He again suggested that the matter should be postponed and said that he had met Mr. Henshall cisually near the land, that they looked at it, and Mr. Henshall had sug- gested that he should send in an offer for it. Upon that, the matter was again postponed. That was the first time that Mr. Heushall's name had been mentioned in the matter to the Town Clerk, Boraugh Treasurer, or any members of the Finance Committee. Mr. Peacock was asked at that meeting if he had brought the matter before the Fire Engine Committee, as he said he would do at the previous meeting, and Mr. Peacock replied that he had. That Was hardly strictly true, but it was not an expression which he (Mr. Swift) was going to cavil at. Mr. Peacock had in fact mentioned it to Alderman Smethurst, the Chairman of the Com- mittee, and to one or two members at a committee meetina, but he had not brought it formally before the Committee as he rather gave the Finance Committee to understand that he had. He said at that meeting that he had brought the matter before the Fire Engine Com- mittee, and that Mr. Henshall had promised to build the cottages upon the land, and the question was again adjourned for another fortnight. On the 15th February Mr. Peacock and Mr. Henshall met in the Market Square, and Mr. Peacock told air. Henshall that he had brought the matter before the Town Clerk, and that it was all right. Mr. Henshall then said he did not think much of the project, and he went hack to his place and wrote forthwith a letter to Mr. Peacock, in which he said : " I have given a lot of consideration to our conversation, but the lion in my path are the conditions you name, anxious as I always am to create business. I feel your proposal ia coatrary to my instincts as to business methods. I have peculiarities which I try to cultivate even when in the way of specu- lating with money, hence my withdrawal from the scheme in its present form." If Mr. Peacock had no ulterior motive and done nothing which was improper, the obvious course was for him to have written to or , seen Mr. Henshall and said : "I don't knew what you mean by your letter. There is nothing improper about the suggestions I have made. If any conditions I have suggested will be imposed are too heavy for you, you bad better come and see if you can get lighter condi- tions from the Council." No notice was,howeveraaken , of the letter and the matter was dropped. That was the end of the proceedings until Alderman Tinnion in March 1905, made the statement in the Council. He had had a conversation with Mr. Henshall in which no names were mentioned, and in per- fect ignorance as to who Was aimed at, he mentioned the matter to the Council. As soon as it had been mentioned, and while Mr. Peacock's name had not been brought into tbe matter at all, Mr. Peacock saw the Town Clerk and told him that he understood that he was the person who was referred to by Alderman Tinnion and had been mentioned by Mr Louis Henshall. He said that if that were so, the transaction related to Flag-lane but there was no truth whatever in Mr. Henshall's statement and if Mr. Henshall dared to repeat what be had obviously told Alderman Tinnion before the Investigation Committee, he would " go for him for all he was worth." Mr. Henshall in spite of that threat, did make a statement to the Investigation Com- mittee—a statement which aas in substance what he (Mr. Swift) had related. In consequence of the fact that Mr. Peacock had identified himself with that con- versation, Mr. Peacock was invited to attend before the committee and was supplied with a copy of Mr. Henshall's statement. On the 13th of July he attended before the Committee and made a statement in answer to the one which had been made by Mr. Henshall. He said : " Yes, I had a conversation with him (Mr. Henshall). I will give you that if you like. Whilst admitting I had a conversation with Mr. Henshall, the statements made by him are totally false and mislead- ing and in my opinion, if he refers to me, done with the purpose of malice. At the outset let me explain, Mr. Henshall and myself have not been on good terms for some years, or since he coupled me through Naylor's as co-defendant with Cross in a County Court case in which I won the day. After I heard the land on Flag-lane was being applied for for private sale by Mr. Henshall and others I objected in committee and proposed the land should be sold by public auction. This was after finding the Corporation could not retain land bought for improvements but must sell. My idea before learning the Corporation's inability toaseep the land was to try and get the Council to erect firemen's dwellings thereon instead of the unsatisfactory housing to-day for these men. Mr. Ilenshall having been told by a member of the Finance Committee that I objected to a private sale asked me why I did so and I explained ptiblio auction is best, and casually pointed out my previous ideas of the Corporation having firemen's cottages there. He asked me for details as to bow any cottages could be profitably erected and I gave him a rough idea on the spot,and he said if cottages could be laid out it would be a good idea irrespective of the fire- men taking the same, as the position was A good one for 55. or Gs. cottages. He suggested the difficulty of laying out and supervising and asked if I was open to superintend, and I told him I would contract to do so for a consideration, details of which could be arranged, but it was not definitely stated for any figure, although I may or may not have said my figure would be about £loo—a proportionate charge as made by me on other building work. ma Henshall states he wrote to me, but whether he did so or not no letter or communication of any description has reached me and Ido not believe one has ever been sent as one would never reach me in that strain without I demanded an explanation of the wording. After the remarks made by Alderman Tinnion at the Council meet- ing Ist once saw the Town Clerk in a straightforward manner, thinking some personal spleen aver Cross's ease might have given Mr. Henshall the opportunity of endeavouring to do me a personal injury. This state- ment is reliable ana my action can be confirmed so far as the Finance Committee is concerned and any remarks T made are perfectly honourable and above board." Mr. Swift aubmitted that this statement confirmed the story which would be told by Mr. Henshall in a rather remarkable way. It confirmed it to this extent at least —that Mr. Peacock suggested or acquiesced in the sug- gestion that he should receive £lOO out of Mr. Henshall's transaction in this Flag-lane property. It confirmed that there had been a conversation and that the Flag-lane property was the subject of it. Tae state- ment was full of inaccuracies. It started off by sug- gesting malice on the part of Mr. Henshall, and the only ground submitted was that some five or six years ago Mr. Peacock was a co-defendant in a County Court case in which Naylor's were the plaintiffs, and that Mr. Henshall was therefore spiteful. If it became necessary to go into that matter, Mr. Henshall would explain that he bad nothing whatever to do with the case, that it was not in his department, and that he did not know until this investigation and not even now except from Mr. Peacock, that Mr. Peacock had anything to do with it or was the same Mr. Peacock who Was joined in the County Court proceedings. It was certainly curious if Mr. Henshall was actuated by malice, that he never mentioned Mr. Peacock's name to a solitary indi- vidual until after the defendant himself made a state- ment to the committee of investigation. alr.Elenshall's statement to the committee never mentioned Mr. Peacock's name but always spoke of bias merely as "a Councillor." Unless Mr. Peacock hid gone forward to the Town Clerk and attendee before the Investiga- tion Committee and admitted the conversation, no one would have known up to now that Mr. Peacock Was I the person in any way referred to by Mr, Henshall. It did not look as though Mr. Henshall were actuated by a very vindictive feeling of malice. Defendant went on to say : " After I heard the land on Flag-lane was being applied for for private sale by Mr. Henshall and others, I objected in committee and proposed the land should be sold by public auction." Mr. Henshall had never applied for the land and had never contemplated applying for it—in fact the only person who had applied was Mr. Hardiman. Mr. Peacock also said : " Mr. afenshsll having been told by a member of the Finance Committee that 1 objected to a private sale askeo me why I did so and I explained public auction is host." Mr. Peacock had not on the 9th j or 10th January objected to a private sale and had never I suggested that it should be sold by public auction. It was not until some date in March that another member of the committee suggested that as there was no firm offer coming forward the land should be put up by auction. On January 9th Mr. Peacock's ()oily action in the matter had been Vs postpone it in order to see whether seine one would come forward who would build cottages for the firemen. He (Mr. Swift) need hardly point out that supposing defendant's story to be absolutely true and that all he was to be paid for was superintending the work,he was committing an offence against the Act. He had no right whatever to suggest soliciting for him- self a fee of £lOO for superintending and at the same time using his position ass member of the Corporation as he undoubtedly Was doing on his own showing,to give information as to the possibility of erecting the houses for the purpose of firemen's cottages. Of course the main part of the story for the prosecution was dependent upon the evidence of; Mr. Henshall, and naturally under the circumstances it was impossible to call two or three witnesses to say they heard the proposition made. When men were bent on improper acts of this character they were done in secret, and even if they were done as part of this was, in the Market Place, they were done in a</p>
        <p>Mr. J. LYON WHITTLE (town clerk), said at a meeting of the Council on or about the 7th of March, 1905 Alderman Ti nni on made a statement to the:Co uncieand in consequence a committee of investigation was appointed by the Council on the 11th Aprill9os.After the appoint- ment he had an interview with the defendant at the Town Hall, Councillor Peacock told him that hounder- stood" that I am the Councillor whom Louis Henshall had referred to, and if ream thelransaction refers to certain property in Flag-lane and Cambria Place. There is no truth in the statement that Henshall has made. The circumstances have been entirely misrepresented and I will go for Henshall for all I am worth if he repeats the statement before the Investigation Committee." Up to the date of the inter- view he had not heard that Mr. Henshall impli- cated Councillor Peacock, whose name had not been mentioned to him in the matter. Ho was present at meetings of the Finance and Estate Committee, Fire Engine Committee and the Town Council held in December 1901, and January, February and March 1905. He believed that at one meeting of the Finance Com- mittee Councillor Peacock mentioned Louis Henshall's ' name as a possible purchaser of the land in Flag-lane. The only time Mr. Henshall's name was mentioned was by Councillor Peacock. At this stage Mr. Rigby Swift called upon the defendant to produce the agenda notice for meetings on the 6th January 1905 and the 20th January 1905, but as defendant failed to produce them they were put in by the Town Clerk, who also produced the Attorney General's flat for the prosecution. Continuing his evidence the Town Clerk stated that Mr. Henshall never approached him with regard to the purchase of the land. At a meeting on the 23rd January he was instructed by the Finance and Estate Committee to endeavour to purchase property belong- ing to Mrs. Hatton, Golborne-street, as it was necessary this should be acquired in order to properly develop the other land. Cross-examined: At that time he attended all the committee meetings. No record was kept to show that he attended the meetings, or his deputy, but he had I notes in his own writing of the meetings on January 9th and 23rd. councillor Peacock had suggested about two years ago that this land was a suitable site for fire- men's dwellings. He believed he had heard the defend- ant advocate the same thing on another occasion out of the committee room. It was not necessary to give notice in order to bring forward a motion at a cons- ' mittee meeting. If the question of building firemen's 1 cottages in committee was not in the shape of a reso- lution it would not appear on the minutes. Mr. Madden : Will you swear then that Mr. Peacock has not frequently brought forward in Committee the matter of firemen's dwellings, but not in the shape of a resolution ? The Town Clerk : I cannot say that. Continuing, he said he was present at the meeting of the Council on March 7th, 1905, at which Alderman Tinnion made a !statement. The Investigation Committee eeas appointed on the 11th April. Mr. Madden : Is it not a fact that before the, Investigation Committee was appointed at all Mr. Peacock came to you and had the conversation with you which you detailed? As a matter of fact did he not come to you within two days from when Alderman Tinnion made the statement in Council? The Town Clerk : He came about the end of April. Mr. Madden : I suggest he saw you within a week. The Town Clerk : I don't think so. I believe it was not until the end of April. Mr. Madden : I put it to you that he saw you before tbe Investigation Committee had been appointed. The Town Clerk: No. He did not. Continuing: A member of the Finance and Estate Committee suggested before the 20th March that the land should be sold by public auction. He could swear that it was not until after the 23rd January. He believed it was made on the 6th February. At one meeting,he believed it was the 23rd January, 1905, defendant asked him if he had had an offer for the laud from Mr. Henshall. A Councillor whom he believed was the defendant, suggested they should purchase the land from Mrs. Hatton. Re-examined: Councillor Peacock, in the interview referred to the Investigation Committee, and therefore the interview must have been after the Investigation. Committee was appointed. Mr. J. FArenunsT(borough treasurer) spoke as to the circumstances relating to the purchase of the land, &amp;c., for public improvement. It was the duty of the Cor- poration to sell surplus land. \Vhen Hardiman's appli- cation was discussed the defendant suggested that fire- men's cottages should be built on the land. He said he would find someone who would make an offer for the land for that purpose. When the matter came up again at the following meeting, defendant asked if any other offer had been received, and whether witness had had an offer from Mr. Louis Henshall. Witness replied that he had not. Mr. Peacock said Mr. Henshall should make an offer, and the matter was adjourned. Subsequently it was decided, on the motion of Councillor Clegg, to sell the land by public auction. Cross-examined: He could not swear that Councillor Clegg moved the sale of the land by auction. To the best of his belief he was the one who did so. Re-examined : He had represented to the Finance Committee that he wished the land to be sold. Coun- cillor Peacock was a member of the Comnittee, and would know all about that. COteNcti.Lort Crum said he was present at the meeting of the Finance Committee on the 9th January. When the question of the sale of the land was raised, Coun- cillor Peacock said he wanted to bring the matter before the Fire Engine Committee, as he wanted that Com- mittee to build firemen's cottages on the land. At is meeting on the 23rd January he asked Counciller Peacock if he had brought the matter before the Fire Engine Committee, and he replied that he had, and the suggestion bad 3 been considered very favourably. At that 'meeting, Councillor Peacock asked whether an offer had been received for the land from Mr. Henshall. COUNCILLOR CLEGG said that at the meeting of the Finance Committee on the 23rd January he asked how Mr. Henshall's name came up, and Councillor Peacock said "I am responsible for that. I was viewing the land and met Mr. Henshall and told him what the Com- mittee were thinking of doing with the lend, and I expected an application here to-day from him." At a meeting of the Committee on February 6th witness pro- posed the resolution that the land should be sold by publie auction. It was not correct that the resolution came about by any action of Councillor Peacock. . Cross-examined : From the time of his election in November 1901 to the 6th February, the proposal to sell the land by publics auction had never been made. IHe had not the least ill-feeling against the defendant. ALDERMAN SMETHURST said that, up to November 1905,he was chairman of the Fire Engine Committee. In February 1905 Councillor Peacock mentioned the subject of firemen's cottages to him in the street, when he suggested that the committee should build houses on vacant land for their men. The proposal was not, how- ever, even brought before the Fire Engine Committee. Mr. Louts HetesHeire st q,ed that onthe 1001 January ,1005 he went from Warrington to Manchester by the 10.55 train. Councillor Peacock and another gentleman, whom he did not know, travelled in the same compartment. Shortly before reaching Manchester Councillor Peacock came to witness's side of the compartment and said he would like to have a chat with him as he had a good speculation in his mind which he wouhl like to bring before witness's notice and asked by what train he would return. Witness said he thought the 12.30, but doubted whether he could do so as his had to go to the docks. Defendant then said he would look out for him. Witness could not catch the 12.30 and when he arrived to catch the 1.30 he found Mr. Peacock waiting for him. Defendant said he had been in time for the 12.30 train but had waited for witness, having taken his lunch to spend the time. They both entered the same compartment in which they were alone throughout the journey. Whilst the train .was in the station they had a gen- eral talk, but as they left Manchester defendant said to witness "We will leave this small talk and come to business." Defendant opened the conversation by saying he knew witness liked to handle a good specula- tion and that he knew of some land in the, heart of the town which was worth his consideration. lie (defendant) knew of things contemplated that would make it a safe thing, and he had only waited to see what he could do with himself but unfortunately he (defendant) could not take it up himself as he was tied up in a pinafore business in Manchester. Having explained this defendant made a suggestion that witness should take it up but suggested that before any details were given he (defendant) must know that he could have his share of the profit, which must not be less £lOO. Witness said this was not an impossibility, but that he could not gauge the value of the information unless' some details web ° given. Defendant then supplied the details. He said the speculation was relative to land and buildings in King-street, near to the market, belonging to the Corporation. The prevailing idea was, said tha defendant, that it was suitable for lock-up, shops, but he knew a better thing—that it was suit- able for firemen's cottages. Defendant explained ,that the Corporation contributed to the firemen's rent anld the scheme that he should press for- ward would be that the Fire Engine Committee should guarantee the rent for a 20 years' lease at ss. per week. It was too good a thing to see thrown away and rather than that should be done he would take it up himself. Defendant further added that the information was cheap at £lOO. Councillor Peacock</p>
        <p>said he had seen the Chairman of the Fire Engine Com- mittee who was favourably impressed with the idea and that witness could take it from him that the scheme would go through, explaining that by his position as vice-chairman of the Committee he could use great influeace to get it through. Up to that time he did not known that the defendant was vice-chairman of the Fire Engine Committee, nor did he know that the Cor- porati iJn had property n Flag-lane to dispose of, or that any suggestion had been made as to the building of firemen's cottages in Flag-lane. After defendant had etplained the scheme witness said that those rosy schemes often failed, and that if he (defend- ant) would break the journey witness would show him that the land was not'so suitable as he seemed to think. They agreed to visit the land on their arrival at Warrington. In the course of the conversation in the train defendant showed him a document. The paper was folded and defendant said it was the committee summons which showed that certain things were coming forward. Defendant said the general idea was .that the land was worth 7d. a yard but some people had an idea it was worth Is. Witness told him that if Is. was the idea of price it blighted the prospects of building cottages as the maximum value of land for cottages was 2d. to 3d. a yard. Defendant said he thought that if witness made an offer of about 3d. or 4d. it might be accepted. He thought the Corporation might favourably consider an offer for the land if it was being taken for the purpose of building firemen's cottages. Defendant said that a bookbinding or printing firm bad applied for the land but that he would block the application. At Warring- ton they left the train together and visited F ag-lane. When they arrived there Councillor Peacock explained an arrangement which the Corporation had made with Clare's, at the bird shop at the corner of Flag-lane and Golborne-street. They then went round the site about which they had been talking. Witness mentioned Hatton's property in Golhorne-street, saying that the land was no use unless the cottages (Hatton's) went with it as they formed the key to the situation. Defendant was uncertain as to whether this belonged to the Corpora- tion or not, and said he would bring it before the Town Clerk and inquire. Witness told Councillor Peacock that he did not think he (defendant) was lending him- self to a good scheme. Defendant said," Why should not a Councillor have the same opportunity of making a bit of money like a private individual ? " As they separated defendant said," Now understand if you hand this over to any third party and P am not party to it (the scheme) I shall block the whole thing." They did not meet again until the 15th of February when witness was passing through the market and defendant hailed him. Defendant said he would have written to him (witness) about the King-street business but he did not know his address. He also said he had seen the Town Clerk and that if witness went to see him he would explain about the Golborne-street property, and he thought if witness went with an offer of 3d. to 4d. per yard the business might go through. Defendant added Don't forget the conditions I named." Witness replied that the matter had not appealed to him and that he would write him. Defendant's only condition was that he was to share the profit which was not to be less than £lOO. The same' day witness wrote to the defendant a letter which he copied in his copy book. Mr. Rigby Swift : I call for that letter. Mr. Madden : We have never received it Continuing witness said he posted it himself. It was directed to 'Councillor Arthur Peacock,Se-tcombe' " and was enclosed in another envelope addressed to Messrs. Peacock Bros., West-street,Warrington.Witness inserted a request on a slip that they should fill in the address of Councillor Peacock who resided at Seacombe. On the 10th of January Councillor Peacock gave witness his address but witness mislaid the envelope upon the back of which it was written. Witness knew Peacock Bros. was a place where the defendant used to go. Witness had never applied for the land, and until a committee of investigation was appointed by the Corporation he had never discussed it with any- one except the defendant. He had never been told by a member of the Finance Committee that the defendant objected to a private sale. Witness did not suggest there was a difficulty in laying out the cottages, and asked Councillor Peacock to superintend the work. He did not know on the 10th January, 1905, that the defendant was in any way connected with the building trade. _</p>
        <p>--Cross-examined : At the date of the conversatiOn he was a traveller for Naylor's. He also speculated on his own behalf. He did not know that defendant was not ' connected with the pinafore business. When he Informed Alderman Tinnion of what had happened he stated it was an instance of a case. He was rather annoyed that Alderman Tinnion had mentioned it, and he was surprised when he saw it in the papers, and he wrote the Alderman a letter. Ile took no notes of the conversation. He wrote out a statement for the Investi- gation Committee. He made his statement to the Committee from notes which he ms.de immediately he saw the matter was likely to become publicly enquired into. The notes were prepared as soon as he knew the Investigation Committee was appointed. Mr. Madden : Are you: certain that there was a "busy bee " with Councillor Peacock in the carriage on the way to Manchester. Witness : Yes. Scraps of their - themselves on my ml of experiences on the ing how they could use the ferries without paying the fares—not tbat they wanted td o so. (Laughter.) Mr. Madden : Did you not may to Mr. Peacock " Why did you block that land?" Witness : No sir.</p>
        <p>Mr. Madden: You know be says he said "Well pnblic auction is the best" and pointed out his ideas as to the Corporation building firemen's cottages. Witness: The question of public auction never arose. Further examined, he said there was nothing said to the effect that Mr. Peacock was to receive £lOO for superintending the building work for him (Mr. Henshall). The statement made by Mr. Peacock to that effect was false. When the scheme was first proposed his (Mr. HenshalPs) attitude was non-com mitts]. Why didn't you repudiate it at once if you thought it was an attempt at bribery. Did you think it was right or proper ?—No. In faut I suggested to him that be was running rather close in making the suggestions. You seem to have been anxious to run with him—No. You went with him to look at the land. Why did you if you thought it dishonest ?—lt was not a case of dishonesty. It was a scheme put before me to oonsider. Did you think it was honest ?—My letter shows. He said he would not give details until he had a ' promise of a share of the profits which was not to be less than £lOO ?—Yes. And he told you of the scheme for building cottages on the land ?—Yes. Did you think this was honest ?—I told himl thought he would find a difficulty in dealing with his own Council as a principle. If he did not find a difficulty were you ready to deal ?—No. '</p>
        <p>Why did you go to the property then ?—To have a look at it. To see what the proposal W(l9. You knew what the proposal was—to give him .£loo— How long did it take you to make your mind un, before you refused to have anything to do with it ?—lt passed out of my mind entirely. If Councillor Peacock had not hailed me in the market I should never have approached him again on the subject. It was dead as far as I was concerned.</p>
        <p>And yet you write and say you have given " a lot of • consideration to it—l had given consideration to his proposal. He stopped me and asked me what I was doing. The £lOO was the lion in your path ?—Yes. Was .2100 too much ?—lt was the substance of the propcsal I objected to, not the amount. So it took you until February 15th, before the sub- stance of the proposal shocked you ?—No. $o far as I was concerned, it had passed out of my mind and if he had not opened the question again I should never have written that letter. One does not like to go and tell a Councillor or any individual straight out that his pro- posal is" this, that and the other. Mr. Madden: "It is contrary to my business instincts." Why did you not say it was contrary to your business instincts on the 10th January. Witness: I simply listened to Councillor Peacock's proposal. I was non-committal. Mr. Madden(reading Mr. Henshall's letter) : "I have peculiarities which I try to cultivate." Did you think much about this letter when you wrote it? Witness : I did. Mr. Madden: You seem to have written it very guardedly. Witness: No sir. Mr. Madden: Did you not write it to save yourself? Witness: No sir. Mr. Madden: Did you not think you had done something shady? Witness: No sir. Mr. Madden: What are your peculiarities? Witness: Honesty. (Laughter.) Mr. Madden : Is that a pecularity ? Witness : As a rule when people are making money they are not particular. ,Jr. Madden : am glad you are amongst the excep- tions. " I have pecularities which I try to cultivate." Did itltake you a month and five days to cultivate these pecularities ? Where you cultivating these pecularities for a month and five days or were they fall grown on the 10th January ? Witness : I would be in the same position on the 10th January as on the 15th February. Mr. Madden: Exactly. That is why it strikes me as funny that you did not say to Councillor Peacock. " Get behind me Satan." This letter is addressed to Councillor Peacock, Seacombe. Witness : He told me in the train he lived across the water. Mr. Madden : Why in:yonr examination did you say New Brighton ? Witness : It is difficult to separate Seacombe and New Brighton. He gave me his address as Seacombe. Mr. Kr (Assistant Borough Surveyor) proved the preparing of plans of the land. ' This concluded the case for the prosecution. Mr. Madden said that if the Bench were of opinion at that Stage that the case must go for trial, be did not propose to put forward his defence. If they thought there was a prima facie case, he did not propose to waste their 'time by addressing them, He, however, asked that if committed the defendant should be sent to the Liverpool Assizes, whieh opened nest week. The prosecution were ready with their case, and there wee</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-05_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>PROSECUTION OF A TOWN COUNCILLOR.</head>
        <p>PROSECUTION OF A TOWN COUNCILLOR.</p>
        <p>A QUESTION OF COMMISSION ON A LAND-PURCHASE.</p>
        <p>Councillor A. Peacock Charged with Corrupt Practice. POLICE COURT PROCEEDINGS.</p>
        <p>The prosecution of .a Warrington Town Councillor under the Corrupt Practices Act, was opened at the local Policerourt yesterday (Thursday) morning. Con- siderable public interest was taken in the case and there was a large attendance of townsmen in the well of the c.ourt. Mr. Charles IVhite presided over the bench, the other magistrates who adjudicated on the case being Messrs. William Richardson, J. Rymer Young, Ad W. Roberts. A number of other justices were present on the bench but they took no part in the case. Among them were Alderman Tinnion, Alderman Francornb, Mr. J. W. Hayward, Mr. H. Thornton, Mr. G. Pilling, and Mr. C. Jackson Holmes. _ .</p>
        <p>Shortly before 10.30 the gentlemen concerned with the case made their appearance. They included the Town Clerk (Mr. J. Lyon Whittle), the Deputy Town Clerk (Mr. A. T. Hallaway), the Borough Treasurer (Mr. John Fairhurst), Mr. Louis Henshall, Mr. Rigby Swift, barrister (instructed for the prosecution), Mr. W. Madden, barrister (instructed by Mr. J. Lt. Evans for the defence), Mr. T. S. Steel, Mr. P. Peacock, and Coun- cillor A. Peacock (the defendant). The summons against Councillor' Peacock was in the following terms : "che information has been laid by Augustus Thornburn Hallaway, Deputy Town Clerk, for and on behalf of the Mayor, aldermen and burgesses of the County Borough, for that you on or about the 10th day of January, 1905, within the said County Borough, unlawfully and cor- ruptly did solicit for yourself a certain gift, fee,reward, or advantage from one Louis Henshall as an inducement to or reward for you using your power and authority in your offices of member of a certain public body, to wit the Town Council of the said County Borough, and of the Finance and Estate and Fire Engine Committees thereof for the procurement of a lease of certain land situate in Flag-lane, within the said County Borough, for the said Louis Henshall, in which the said public body was concerned, contrary to the provisions of sub- section 1 of section 1 of the Public Bodies Corrupt Practices Act, 1899."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-05_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>STATEMENT FOR THE PROSECUTION.</head>
        <p>THE STATEMENT FOR THE PROSECUTION.</p>
        <p>Mr. Rigby Swift, in opening the case,said he appeared on behalf of the Borough Council of Warrington to prose- cute Arthur Peacock on an information which had been laid by the Deputy-Town Clerk by the consent and under the flat of the Attorney General. The offence was one which was not often charged against a person and it was an offence which anybody taking an interest in public life must be 'gratified was not often charged. But where a case was brought in which a sufficiency of evidence appeared to justify putting a person upon his trial for having committed such an offence it was undoubtedly the duty of any public body of which he was a member to take proceedings and to lay the whole of the facts as far as they could be ascertained before a Court of Law in order that it might determine whether in fact, there hsd or had not been an offence committed under the Section. If the Bench after deliberating on the evidence came to the conclusion that there was a prima facie case made out against the defendant— not that he was necessarily guilty but that there was such a prima facie case as indicated that the matter should be inquired into by a Court with a jury,—he would invite them on behalf of the Crown to commit the defendant to the next Quarter Session + which would be held on the 29th inst in order that he might take his trial on charges which would be formulated on the evidence. The circumstances under which the case attained a measure of publicity, and which led up to the pro- ceedings which had been taken, were that on the 7th March 1905 one of the Warrington Councillors—Alder- man Tinnion—in the Council Chamber mentioned the fact that he had had a conversation with a gentleman, whose name he did not give, with reference to certain negotiations which that gentleman had had with a Coun- cillor whose name also was not mentioned with regard to the purchase of certain land. The substance of that conversation as repeated to the Council by Alderman Tinnion was that the Councillor was to receive a fee of £lOO, provided that a scheme which he suggested to the third party was carried through, with his assistance, various committees of the Council. In consequence of that statement the Council decided to appoint a committee to investigate the matter to see whether anything had been done which affected the honour of the Council, and to report to the Council. That committee be believed, met on eight occasions and as a result Of its inquiries it laid certain informa- tion before the Council who having taken opinions upon it from legal authorities, ultimately submitted the conclusions to the Attorney General who gave his fiat for the prosecution. Sub Section 1 of the Public Bodies Corrupt Practices Act of 1899 provided that , "any person who shall by himself or by or in con- junction with any other person corruptly solicit or receive or agree to receive for himself or for any other person any gift, loan, fee or advantage whether as an inducement to or reward for or other- wise on account of any member, officer, or servant of a public body as in this Act defined,doing or forbearing to do anything in respect of any matter or transaction whatsoever, actual or proposed, in which the said public body is concerned, shall be guilty of a misdemeanour." The defendant had been a member of the Town Council since 1897 and he had since November 9th, 1903 been a member of the Finance and Estates Committee. in addition he was from Novem- ber 1897 to December 1905 a member of the Fire Engine Committee and was therefore fully acquainted with the transactions of both committees. In 1901 the Corpora- tion were possessed of certain lands ia Flag-lane which had been acquired for the purposes of street widening. Under section 175 of the Public Health Act of 1875 it was necessary where an Urban Council had acquired landlfor public improvements that after they had utilized as much of the land as was required for the improve- ment they should dispose of the surplus land at the best possible price. Consequently at the end of 1904, the Corporation found themselves with a strip of land in Flag lane which had not been required for improve- ment purposes and it was necessary that they should dispose of it at the best possible price. In December, 1904, the Borough Treasurer was approached bya gentle- man named Hardiman who was [anxious to acquire a portion of the land in order that he might erect a build- ing in which to carry on his business of a printer. Mr. Hardiman's ; proposal came before the Finance and Estates Committee on January 9th, 1905. A notice convening the meeting was sent to the members and the agenda paper disclosed the fact that the question of this land would be one of the subjects for discusaion. Councillor Peacock received a notice and attended the meeting. He took a prominent part in the discussion with regard to the land, explaining that he thought the land was too good for the purpose of erecting a workshop upon it and that ballad a scheme in his head the value of whieh was probably great— certainly he (Mr. Swift) did not propose to adversely criticise it—that the land should be utilized for the erection of cottages for the firemen, it being in close proximity to the Fire Station. His proposal was that tho Fire Engine Committee should. guarantee the rent for a certale number of years— twenty he believed—and that the firemen should occupy the houses instead of being scattered in aoarions places. He (Mr. Swift) did not think there could he any doubt that Mr. Peacock held that the pro- posal would be a reasonable one and one which he was anxioua to carry through. ge (Mr. Swift) ventured to think he would show the Berich one very considerable reason which weighed with the defendant in his idea to carry it through. The only result of Mr. Peacock's suggestion on January 9th was that the committee resolved to have a plan prepared of the premises. Then with the knowledge of what was in the committee's mind, of the fact that the committee had been approached by Mr. Flardiman and that the Borough Treasurer thought that a shilling a yard should be paid for the land, Mr. Peacock on the day after the meeting —Jannary 10th —went to Manchester. He travelled with some acquaintance of his whose name they could , not tell, and there happened also to get into the carriage at Warrington Mr. Louis Henshall, who was connec- ted with a large firm of timber merchants in Warrington known as Naylor's. Mr. Henshall had from time to ' time speculated in land, and had on occasions financed builders. Although he (Mr. Swift) could not prove it, he thought it was fair thi.t the Bench should assume that Mr. Peacock was acquainted more or less with Mr. Henshall's general reputation as a man who would if a good thing MI O shown to him go in for it. As they were approaching Manchester Mr. Peacock left the side of the carriage on which his friend was sitting and camo and sat down by Mr. Henshall. He said : " I want to have a little chat with you. I have got a good thing on—a speculation [ think, which might turn out well. When will you be coming back again to Warrington ?" Mr. Henshall explained that he had to go down to the Docks at Manchester, that he was hoping to catch the 12.30 train but that he might be delayed. As a matter of fact, he was delayed, and did not get back to the station until L3O. Mr. Peacock was on the station and he stated that he had come down in plenty of time for the 12.30 train but he thought he would wait and see if Mr. Henshall turned up by 1.30 in the time by having lunch). They got into a carriage together and as soon as the train got out of the station Mr. Peacock came to the business which he had in hand. He (Mr. Swift) was not going to endeavour to detail the whole of the conversation as the bench would receive it from Mr. Hensball. The 'substance of it was that Mr. Peacock knew that the Finance Committee were going to sell a piece of land in Flag-lane, also that it was suitable for firemen's cottages and that in all probability the Fire</p>
        <p>•</p>
      </div>
