      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-05_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Preachers for Sunday. (Continued from Page 3.) Wycliffe Church : 10.30 and 6.30. Rev. John Yonge. Bold-street Wesleyan Church : 10.30, Rev. S. Pitt 6.30, Rev. Wm. Ifellor. Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church, Sunday Schoo Anniversary : 10.30 and 6.30, Rev. J. Grange Bennett, Connexional evangelist. 2.30: Musical service by the children and choir. Speaker, Councillor Brough. Golborne-street Baptist Church : 10.30, Rev. H. Dunnico ("Guiding hand of Doi.") 6.30, Rev. H. Dunnico ("Are Socialists atheists ?") Latchford Baptist Church : 10.30, Rev. J. F. Matthews (" Jew, traitor,, sinner.") 6.0. Rev. J. F. Matthews ("The self-consciousness of Jesus.")</p>
        <p>Rev. H. Dunnico's Decision. - Addressing a crowded congregation at GoThorne- street Baptist Church, on Sunday evening, the Rev. H. Dunaico, the pastor, who has received a call to Ken- sington Baptist Church, Liverpool, said he had given the matter his careful and prayerful consideration, and had decided to accept the call, and to close his ministry at Warrington on the 24th of Juno. He had accepted the call, not because he wished to leave War- rington, or because his work luid not been successful. They all knew it had been successful. He would remember his four years' connection with the church with the utmost pleasure. He had gained a large number of warm, devoted and loyal friends, and the severance to him,at any rate,would cause him great pain. He had a narrow trust deed and much narrower minds to contend with, and he had felt for some time that he could not build up a strong Christian church as he understood that term. So far as the public and the great majority of the Church were concerned, he had nothing to complain of. He was devoutly thankful that the working men of Warrington had manifested their loyalty and devotion to him in a marked degree. Although he was leaving he should not be quite a stranger to them. He was not going far away and he should take as much interest as possible in the work of the town wherever God might lead him. He should go' away thinking of good old Warrington, and pray for it, and,perhaps,sorne day he might be able to repay to them in some measure the debt he owed them for all the experience he had gained in their midst.</p>
        <p>' ,</p>
      </div>
