      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-05-05_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <p>no reason why Councillor Peacock should have the case hanging over his head for a month when the case could be ready to go before the Crown Jury at the Assizes on Monday. Mr. Rigby Swift said it was practically a physics/ impossibility to present the case at the Assizes. They could not get the depositions copied and seat to the Judge, and get the Clerk of the Assize to draw up the indictment before Monday. It could not possibly be done.</p>
        <p>The magistrates then retired and on their return after a few minutes absence the Chairman said they had come to the opinion with very great regret considering the position which the defendant occupied, but they were convinced that the defendant must take his trial at the next Liverpool Quarter Sessions. They did not think it possible to get the case ready for the Assizes nor did they think it a case suitable for the Assizes. Councillor Peacock reserved his defence.</p>
        <p>No objection was raised to an application for bait which was granted in the defendant's own surety of £lOO.</p>
      </div>
